  // What each kind of Domain was, for the line under its name ("A Commerce Domain: ...").
  var DKIND = {
    Commerce: 'Markets, ports and caravan cities. Whoever held the market was paid by everyone who used it.',
    Industry: 'Workshops, shipyards, mines and kilns. They made ships, arms, books and tools for less than anyone else could.',
    Thought: 'Libraries, schools and observatories. They gave knowledge, and teachers whose students carried it on.',
    Land: 'Walls, fortresses and passes. They were ground nobody could be pushed off.',
    Culture: 'Theatres, temples, monuments and games. People came, watched, believed and remembered.',
    Law: 'Capitals, courts and council fires. What was decided there held everywhere else.',
    Routes: 'Roads, straits and sea lanes. Whoever held the way could be anywhere first.'
  };
  // The 75 Domains. cap, ll, fact as for a card; sure: how sure the fact is (3 well attested, 2 the usual account,
  // 1 tradition, and the sentence says so; from scripts/anoria/places/domains.json). art: the picture's brief; bs:
  // how sure the brief is (3 the place stands or was dug and recorded, and the brief keeps to that; 2 a
  // reconstruction on the usual account; 1 little is known of how it looked, and the brief stays general).
  // scripts/anoria/domains.txt says which were checked against a text and which rest on memory.
  // The first twelve were the game's Places and keep their pictures and briefs. tx0: the rule each of them had on
  // the old fixed map, which a lesson's table and a game saved before Domains still show. A Domain's rule today is
  // DRULE[its rule word], which the page reads through cards.js (ui.js info()).
  var PLACES = {
    // Commerce
    malacca: { cap: 'Malay Peninsula, founded about 1400', ll: [102.25, 2.19], fact: 'Its harbor commanded the strait between the Indian Ocean and the China seas, and a visitor in the 1510s counted 84 languages spoken in its market.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The harbor of Malacca about 1480, under its sultans, seen from a boat in the river mouth: timber houses on stilts with palm-thatch roofs crowd both banks, a wooden bridge with stalls on it joins them, and on the low hill behind stands a large timber hall with tiered roofs. Ships of three kinds lie at anchor: a Chinese junk with ribbed mat sails, a dhow from Gujarat with one slanting sail, and a broad Javanese trader with two steering oars. In a small boat in front, three people, each different: the harbor master, a Malay official in a short jacket, a wrapped skirt and a folded headcloth, counting bundles; a Gujarati merchant in a white turban holding a sample of cloth; and a Chinese sailor with a jar on his shoulder. No stone fort, no church, no cannon: the Portuguese have not come. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    kilwa: { cap: 'Tanzania, at its height about 1300', ll: [39.51, -8.96], fact: 'Its sultans controlled the gold trade of the East African coast and struck their own coins, and Ibn Battuta, who came in 1331, called it one of the most beautiful towns he had seen.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'Kilwa Kisiwani about 1330, seen from the beach below the sultan\'s palace of Husuni Kubwa: white walls of coral stone with pointed arches and low domes along the top of a bluff about five times a man\'s height, with a stair cut down the rock to the water. A trading ship of the coast, its planks sewn together with cord and its one square sail woven of matting, lies in the green shallows. On the sand, three people, each different: a merchant of the town in a long cotton robe and a wound turban weighing gold dust in a small hand balance, a sailor wading ashore with an elephant tusk on his shoulder, and a boy with a basket of fish. Mangroves at the edge. No minaret towers, no cannon, no European ship. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    venice: { cap: 'The Adriatic, at its height 1100 to 1500', ll: [12.336, 45.438], fact: 'For some four hundred years its merchants held most of Europe\'s trade in spices and silk with the East.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'Venice about 1495, at the Rialto, as a painting of that year shows it: the Grand Canal crowded with slim black gondolas, and across it a wooden bridge on timber piles with a drawbridge in the middle that lifts to let masted boats through. The houses on both sides have pointed-arched windows and tall chimneys that widen at the top like funnels. On the quay in front, bales and barrels marked with merchants\' signs. Three people, each different: a Venetian merchant in a long black gown and a small black cap checking a ledger, a gondolier in a short jacket and striped hose leaning on his oar, and a trader from the eastern Mediterranean in a white turban and a long coat with a sack of pepper at his feet. No stone bridge, no carnival masks, no motor boats. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    lisbon: { cap: 'Portugal, the spice port from about 1500', ll: [-9.14, 38.71], fact: 'From about 1500 the king\'s India House here took in every cargo of pepper and spice that came round Africa, and for a time Lisbon was the richest port in Europe.', sure: 2, bs: 2, art: 'The waterfront of Lisbon about 1515: the wide River Tagus with three high-sided ships at anchor, each with a red cross on its square mainsail, and lighters rowing sacks ashore. On the quay stands the king\'s new palace, a long stone building with an arcade at ground level, where the cargoes are weighed. Three people at a great beam balance, each different: a long iron beam hangs at its middle from the top of a tall wooden tripod, and from each end of the beam a flat pan hangs by three chains, one pan holding iron weights and the other the pepper: a royal clerk in a dark gown and a flat cap, noting a weight in a ledger; a barefoot porter tipping black peppercorns from a sack into the pan; and a sailor, thin and sunburnt from the voyage, sitting on a bale. Gulls, masts, a hill of white houses behind. No statues, no trams, no later domes. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    carthage: { cap: 'Tunisia, founded about 814 BC', ll: [10.323, 36.853], fact: 'Its merchant fleets controlled the trade of the western Mediterranean, and its round harbor had sheds for some two hundred warships to guard it.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The harbors of Carthage about 200 BC, seen from high above and to one side, as an ancient account and the excavations give them: a perfectly round basin of still water with a round island in its middle, the island and the whole shore ringed with ship sheds, each opening fronted by a pair of stone columns, so that the ring looks like one long portico. The round harbor is about nine times as wide as a warship is long. A narrow channel joins it to a long rectangular harbor full of broad merchant ships, and beyond that is the open sea. On the island stands the admiral\'s tower. Only three figures can be made out, on the tower\'s roof: the harbor commander and two signalmen with a horn. One war galley is being hauled up a slip by ropes. The city rises white behind. No Roman buildings, no lighthouse. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    firenze: { cap: 'Tuscany, the florin from 1252', ll: [11.256, 43.77], fact: 'Its gold florin, first struck in 1252, was trusted across Europe, and its bankers lent money to kings and popes.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'Florence about 1470, at a money changer\'s table in the open market: a bench covered with green cloth, with stacks of small gold coins, a pair of coin scales and a thick ledger. Above the roofs behind rise the plain stone tower of the town hall with its jutting, battlemented top, and the great red-tiled dome of the cathedral with its white ribs. Three people, each different: a banker in a long red gown and a cloth hood rolled into a cap, weighing one coin against a small weight; a wool merchant in a fur-edged coat waiting with a purse in his fist; and a boy with a satchel of letters, about to run. The coins are too small to show their design. No statue in the square, no tourists, no carriage. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    timbuktu: { cap: 'Mali, 14th century', ll: [-3.0, 16.77], tx0: 'Figures you play here cost 1 less.', fact: 'It grew rich where the salt caravans of the Sahara met the gold boats of the Niger, and its wealth paid for its scholars and libraries.', sure: 3, art: 'Timbuktu: a great mud-brick mosque with wooden beams studding its towers, scholars with books in the square, camels and traders, desert sky.' },
    tlatelolco: { cap: 'Valley of Mexico, 1337 to 1521', ll: [-99.137, 19.451], fact: 'The great market of the Aztec capital drew some sixty thousand buyers and sellers a day, by the count of the Spaniards who saw it in 1519.', sure: 2, bs: 2, art: 'The great market of Tlatelolco about 1510, as the first Spaniards described it: a wide paved square with goods laid out on reed mats under cloth shades, in ordered rows by kind: heaps of maize and chillies, cacao beans, folded cotton cloaks, obsidian blades, live turkeys, bundles of bright feathers. Beyond the square rises a stepped pyramid with two temples side by side on its top, about eighteen times a man\'s height. Three people in front, each different: a woman kneeling at a mat of tomatoes and squash, counting cacao beans into a buyer\'s hand; a porter bent under a load carried by a band across his forehead; and a long-distance merchant with a fan and a staff, a bundle of green quetzal feathers under his arm. A canal with two canoes at the edge. No horses, no wheels, no coins, no Spaniards. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    petra: { cap: 'Jordan, c. 300 BC', ll: [35.44, 30.33], tx0: 'Figures here can\'t be targeted by enemy Deeds.', fact: 'The Nabataeans grew rich here taxing the incense caravans from Arabia, and carved their tombs into the rose-red cliffs.', sure: 3, art: 'Petra: a columned temple front carved into a rose-red cliff, glimpsed at the end of a narrow shadowed canyon, a rider entering.' },
    djenne: { cap: 'Mali, a market from about 800', ll: [-4.555, 13.906], fact: 'For centuries the gold, salt and grain of West Africa changed hands in its market on the Niger, and its traders worked hand in hand with Timbuktu\'s.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The river port of Djenné about 1450, late in the afternoon in the dry season, with a hot dusty wind blowing and a low orange sun raking across the town. The town stands on a low island in the flood plain of the Niger, with flat bare marsh round it and no trees but a few palms far off. Its houses are true Sudano-Sahelian mud architecture, built of round sun-dried mud bricks and plastered smooth with mud, every edge soft and rounded: a waterfront of separate one- and two-storey houses, each a little different, with flat roof terraces. Each house front is decorated with rounded vertical buttresses built into the wall, only a hand or two thick, that rise just above the top of the wall as small rounded pinnacles, each pinnacle no taller than a child; short palm-wood beams (toron) stick out of the walls in rows; a tall doorway framed by two buttresses, with a heavy plank door; few, small windows; short wooden water spouts jut from the roofs. The pinnacles are small and belong to the house fronts: no free-standing cones, no huge spikes, no building much taller than the others, no mosque, no ladders, no square blocks, no pueblo look. Long plank canoes with pointed ends are drawn up on the mud bank below the town, and one is being unloaded. Three people, each different, small and low at the left of the picture: a boatman with a pole standing in a canoe; a trader from the desert in a dark blue robe and a veil wound round his head, holding one single flat gray slab of rock salt, broad and flat like a stone tablet, as long as his arm and as thick as a hand, bound round with cord, the only salt in the picture; and a woman of the town in a wrapped cloth, with a tall calabash of rice on her head, her arm stretched out and her index finger pointing straight at the slab of salt as she bargains. Cattle egrets on the bank. The famous mosque of today was built in 1907 and is not shown. No camels in the water, no cars. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    // Industry
    longjiang: { cap: 'Nanjing, working from 1405', ll: [118.72, 32.06], fact: 'The Ming shipyards here on the Yangtze built the great ships of Zheng He\'s fleets from 1405, and some of their docks can still be seen.', sure: 2, bs: 2, art: 'The treasure shipyard at Nanjing about 1410: a seagoing junk being built in a long basin dug beside the Yangtze, shut off from the river by an earth dam. The unfinished hull stands on timber blocks, about five times a man\'s height from keel to rail, with a flat bow, a high flat stern and a row of solid plank walls across its inside that divide it into separate holds. Bamboo scaffolding stands along one side. On the ground in front lies a rudder post as long as six men lying end to end. Three people, each different: an old master shipwright with a carpenter\'s square and an ink line, a caulker driving fiber and lime putty into a seam with a mallet, and a young sawyer resting on a plank. The size of the largest ships is disputed, so this one is large, not impossible. No sails yet, no dragons, no fleet. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    laurion: { cap: 'Attica, a great strike in 483 BC', ll: [24.05, 37.71], fact: 'A rich strike of silver here in 483 BC paid for the two hundred warships with which Athens beat the Persians at Salamis.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The silver mines of Laurion in Attica about 480 BC, at an ore washery on a dry hillside above the sea: a sloping floor of fitted stone with water running along a shallow channel from a plastered tank into round settling basins. Beside it a square shaft drops into the rock, with a wooden windlass and a rope over it. Three workers, each different, all thin, sunburnt and in rough tunics, for the mines were worked by slaves: a man with a wooden rake spreading crushed ore under the running water, a woman grinding ore in a stone trough, and an old man hauling a leather bucket up the shaft. An overseer\'s cloak and a set of scales lie on a wall. Pines, thorn bushes, glare. No temple, no soldiers, no coins in the air. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    damascus: { cap: 'Syria, famous for blades by about 900', ll: [36.29, 33.51], fact: 'Its name went on blades of patterned steel, forged from ingots brought from India, that were prized from Spain to Persia.', sure: 2, bs: 2, art: 'A swordsmith\'s shop in the covered market of Damascus about the year 900: a deep stone-vaulted booth open to the street, with a charcoal forge, a leather bellows and an anvil set in a tree stump. The smith, a lean man of fifty in a leather apron and a wound turban, holds a straight, two-edged blade up to the daylight, and its steel shows fine wavy lines like running water. On the bench lie three small round cakes of steel, each the size of a fist, brought from India. Two others, each different: a boy working the bellows, and a buyer in a long striped robe testing the edge of a finished blade with his thumb. Through the arch at the end of the street, a square stone minaret. No curved sabers, no guns, no carpets on the walls. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    meroe: { cap: 'Sudan, a royal city from about 300 BC', ll: [33.75, 16.94], fact: 'The kings of Kush ruled from this city on the Nile, and the great heaps of slag left by its iron furnaces can still be seen.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'Meroë on the Nile about 100 BC. In front, an iron furnace at work in a sunken yard: a cylinder of baked clay about as tall as a man\'s chest, glowing at its base, fed with air by pairs of clay pot bellows worked with sticks, with a heap of black slag beside it. Two smelters of Kush, each different, in white linen kilts: an old master judging the color of the flame, and a young man pumping the bellows. A third person, a woman with a basket of charcoal on her head, walks toward them. Behind them, across the plain, stands the royal burial ground: a cluster of steep, narrow pyramids of dark sandstone, each a different size, the tallest about fifteen times a man\'s height, each with a small chapel on its east side. No smooth Egyptian pyramids, no sphinx, no camels. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    mainz: { cap: 'The Rhine, printing from about 1450', ll: [8.27, 50], fact: 'Gutenberg\'s workshop in Mainz printed about 180 Bibles around 1455, and within fifty years presses across Europe had turned out millions of books.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The upper room of Gutenberg\'s workshop in Mainz about 1455, where the printed sheets of the Bible are finished: a long attic with lines strung from beam to beam, and on them dozens of large sheets hung to dry, each printed in two columns of black letter. Three people at a long table, each different: a woman folding dry sheets and gathering them into sections, a gray-haired man with a fine brush painting a red initial letter into a blank space in the text, and a boy carrying a fresh damp sheet at arm\'s length. Through the open door at the far end, down a stair, the wooden screw of the press can just be seen. The letters on the sheets are too small to read. No bound book with a title, no portrait of the master, no modern type. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    ironbridge: { cap: 'Shropshire, iron from 1709', ll: [-2.485, 52.627], fact: 'Abraham Darby first smelted iron with coke in this valley in 1709, and the world\'s first iron bridge, cast there, has stood since 1779.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'The Iron Bridge over the River Severn in Shropshire about 1790, at night, seen from the riverbank below: one great arch of dark cast iron leaping the river, made of five slender ribs side by side with iron circles in the corners, the span as long as seventeen men lying end to end, on stone piers. Under it passes a flat-bottomed river barge with its mast lowered, loaded with iron bars. Behind the bridge the gorge glows orange from the furnaces at Coalbrookdale, with flame at a furnace mouth and smoke rolling up. Three people, each different: a bargeman poling at the stern, a boy at the bow with a lantern, and a woman with a basket crossing the bridge above, lit from behind. No railway, no steel cables, no chimneys taller than the gorge. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    jingdezhen: { cap: 'Jiangxi, imperial kilns from 1004', ll: [117.18, 29.27], fact: 'For a thousand years its kilns made porcelain for China\'s emperors and for buyers from Japan to Europe, who could not copy it for centuries.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'A porcelain workshop at Jingdezhen about 1430, under the Ming: open-sided sheds with tiled roofs around a yard, a long brick kiln with a glowing fire mouth behind them, and stacks of round clay boxes in which the pots are fired. Three people, each different: a potter at a low wheel set in the ground, drawing up the wall of a white bowl with wet hands; an old painter on a stool with a fine brush, painting a blue-black scroll of leaves onto an unfired white jar that he steadies on his knee; and a young carrier crossing the yard with a long plank on his shoulder, on which a dozen unfired bowls stand in a line. White dust on everything. No finished blue-and-white vase in a showy pose, no dragon, no foreign buyers. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    benin: { cap: 'Nigeria, a kingdom from about 1200', ll: [5.62, 6.335], fact: 'The guild of bronze casters has worked for the Obas of Benin since about the 13th century, and their plaques kept the kingdom\'s history on the palace walls.', sure: 2, bs: 2, art: 'The brass casters\' quarter of Benin City about 1550, beside the Oba\'s palace: a yard with a clay furnace sunk in the ground and a pair of bowl bellows covered with hide. Behind it runs a palace gallery whose wooden pillars are covered from top to bottom with cast brass plaques, and on its roof stands a turret with a great brass bird on its top and a brass snake running down its front, as an account of the 1660s and the plaques of the palace show it. Three members of the casters\' guild, each different: a master with gray in his hair lifting a small glowing crucible with iron tongs to pour into a clay mold set in the earth, a young man working the bellows sticks, and an elder holding a finished plaque up to the light to judge it. Dignified, skilled work. No masks worn, no spears, no huts of straw. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    // Thought
    library: { cap: 'Alexandria, founded c. 285 BC', ll: [29.91, 31.21], tx0: 'Whoever holds it draws a card each round.', fact: 'The kings of Egypt set out to gather every book in the world here, and by one ancient account books found on ships in the harbor were taken and copied.', sure: 2, art: 'The great hall of the Library of Alexandria, towering shelves of scrolls, scholars on ladders, sunbeams through high windows, the harbor lighthouse visible through an arch.' },
    greenwich: { cap: 'London, founded 1675', ll: [-0.0015, 51.4769], fact: 'It was founded in 1675 to solve the problem of finding longitude at sea, and since 1884 the world\'s maps and clocks have been set from its meridian.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'The Octagon Room of the Royal Observatory at Greenwich about 1680, as an engraving of the time shows it: a tall eight-sided room with dark wood panelling and long narrow windows more than twice a man\'s height, open to the night sky. A refracting telescope as long as three men lying end to end rests on a ladder-like stand and points out of a window; a brass quadrant on a wheeled frame stands beside it; and two clock dials are set into the panelling, their long pendulums hidden behind it. Three people, each different: the astronomer, a thin man in a long gown and a full wig, with his eye at the telescope; an assistant reading a clock by candlelight and calling the time; a boy writing the figures in a book. No red time ball, no brass line on the floor, no electric light. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    akademeia: { cap: 'Athens, founded about 387 BC', ll: [23.708, 37.992], fact: 'Plato taught here from about 387 BC, and Aristotle came as a student, stayed twenty years and went on to teach Alexander.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'Plato\'s Academy about 360 BC: a grove of old olive and plane trees outside the walls of Athens, with a simple colonnade along one side, a small altar to the Muses, a sundial on a short column and a globe of the heavens in an open box, as an ancient mosaic of the Academy shows. Three people on a shaded path, each different: an old teacher in a plain cloak seen from behind, walking slowly with a staff; a student of about thirty walking backward in front of him, arguing with both hands; and a boy crouching to draw a square and its diagonal in the dust with a stick. The Acropolis is small and pale in the distance. No writing over a gate, no scrolls flying, no marble lecture hall. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    padua: { cap: 'Veneto, founded 1222', ll: [11.877, 45.407], fact: 'Copernicus studied here and Galileo taught here for eighteen years, and its anatomy theater of 1595 is the oldest still standing.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'The anatomy theater of the University of Padua, built in 1595, seen from the floor looking straight up: six steep oval galleries of carved walnut railings rise one above another like the inside of a funnel, each so narrow that a person can only stand, around a small table at the bottom. The whole room is no wider than five men lying end to end. Candles on the rails light it. Three people, each different: a professor in a black gown and a square cap at the table with an open book and a pointer, and two students leaning over the rails at different heights, one writing, one craning to see. The table holds only the book, a candle and a covered tray. Warm candlelight on dark wood. No blood, no skeleton, no modern lamps. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    wisdom: { cap: 'Baghdad, about 830', ll: [44.36, 33.34], fact: 'Under the caliphs of Baghdad, scholars turned the books of Greece, Persia and India into Arabic and built on them.', sure: 2, bs: 1, art: 'A room of translators in Baghdad about 830, under the caliph al-Ma\'mun. What the House of Wisdom looked like is not known, so the room is plain: whitewashed walls with deep niches in which books lie flat in stacks, reed mats, low desks, daylight from a courtyard door. Three scholars, each different in age, face and dress: a Christian physician from Syria in a dark robe reading aloud from an old Greek book with wooden covers; an Arab scribe sitting cross-legged, writing what he hears on a sheet of paper with a reed pen, an inkwell at his knee; and a Persian astronomer in a tall cap turning a small brass astrolabe in his fingers while he listens. Through the door, far off, a green dome above the round city. No telescope, no globe, no printed pages. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    nalanda: { cap: 'Bihar, about 427 to 1200', ll: [85.444, 25.136], fact: 'For some seven hundred years students came from as far as China and Korea to study at this monastery, and the pilgrim Xuanzang wrote of thousands of them.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The monastery of Nalanda about 640, when the Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang studied there: a courtyard of red brick with rows of monks\' cells behind a covered walk, a well, and beyond it a great brick temple with a broad stair up its front and a tower at each corner, about seventeen times a man\'s height. Three people under a tree in the courtyard, each different: an old Indian teacher with a shaved head, his robe leaving the right shoulder bare, seated on a low stool with a narrow palm-leaf book on his knees; a young Indian monk listening with his chin in his hand; and a pilgrim monk from China in a fuller, long-sleeved robe, writing notes. No gold statues, no prayer flags, no ruins. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    royalsociety: { cap: 'London, founded 1660', ll: [-0.13, 51.506], fact: 'Founded in London in 1660, it printed Newton\'s Principia, and the journal it began in 1665 is the oldest science journal still published.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'A meeting of the Royal Society at Gresham College in London about 1665: a panelled room with a long table, and on it an air pump, a brass cylinder with a crank handle on a wooden frame, topped by a glass globe as big as a man\'s head. Inside the globe a lit candle is going out as the air is drawn off. A silver-gilt mace lies on the table. Three people, each different: a small, stooped man seen from behind, working the crank; a stout fellow in a long dark wig leaning in with a magnifying glass; and a younger one writing the result in a large book. Candlelight and window light. No bird in the globe, no portrait on the wall that can be recognized, no electric apparatus. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    seneca: { cap: 'New York, 1848', ll: [-76.8, 42.91], tx0: 'A quiet Place with no power of its own.', fact: 'The first women\'s rights convention in the United States met here in 1848, and its declaration that all men and women are created equal was taken up across the country.', sure: 3, art: 'A brick chapel hall in a small 1840s American town, a packed meeting inside, a woman reading from a paper at the front, horse carts outside.' },
    // Land
    gibraltar: { cap: 'The Strait of Gibraltar, held from 711', ll: [-5.35, 36.14], fact: 'Whoever holds the Rock watches the only way between the Mediterranean and the Atlantic, and from 1779 it withstood a siege of more than three and a half years.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'Inside a gun gallery tunnelled through the north face of the Rock of Gibraltar during the Great Siege, 1782: a rough-hewn tunnel of pale limestone a little taller than a man, lit from one square opening cut through the cliff. An iron cannon on a wooden carriage points down through the opening, and through it, far below, lie the flat sandy neck of land, the enemy\'s siege lines as thin pale strokes, and the blue bay. Three gunners of the garrison, each different, in red coats with their sleeves rolled: a gray sergeant sighting along the barrel, a big man with a rammer, and a boy carrying a powder charge in a leather case. Smoke drifts out of the opening. No monkeys, no modern town, no flags. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    wall: { cap: 'Northern China', ll: [116.57, 40.43], tx0: 'Figures defending here get +1 Might.', fact: 'Its walls were built and rebuilt over about two thousand years to hold China\'s northern frontier.', sure: 3, art: 'The Great Wall of China snaking over steep mountain ridges, watchtowers with signal fires, banners in a hard wind.' },
    thermopylae: { cap: 'Central Greece, 480 BC', ll: [22.54, 38.8], fact: 'In 480 BC the pass between the mountain and the sea was so narrow that a few thousand Greeks held back the Persian army here for days.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The pass of Thermopylae in the summer of 480 BC, empty before the battle, seen along the track: on the left a wall of gray cliff and steep scrub going up out of the picture, on the right the sea coming right up to the track, and between them a strip of ground no wider than eight men lying end to end. A low, rough wall of piled stones with one gap crosses it. Steam rises from hot springs that run in milky streams across the path. Only one person: a Greek lookout sitting on the wall with his back to us, a round shield leaning beside him and a spear across his knees, watching the far end of the pass, where dust is rising. Still, hot and waiting. No army, no fighting, no monument. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    himeji: { cap: 'Japan, rebuilt 1609', ll: [134.69, 34.84], fact: 'Its gates and walled paths wind like a maze to confuse an attacker, and in four hundred years it has never been destroyed by war, fire or earthquake.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'Himeji Castle about 1620, seen from inside its lower defenses, on a path that climbs between high walls: sloping bases of fitted gray stone, white plastered walls with rows of small square and round shooting holes, and a heavy timber gate plated with iron where the path turns back on itself. Above the walls the main keep rises white, with five tiers of gray tiled roofs and curved gables, standing with its stone base about twenty-five times a man\'s height. Three people on the path, each different: an official of the lord\'s household in a stiff-shouldered jacket and wide trousers with two swords, and two foot soldiers with spears and flat lacquered hats guarding the gate. No cherry blossom, no tourists, no cannon. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    laferriere: { cap: 'Northern Haiti, built 1805 to 1820', ll: [-72.244, 19.573], fact: 'Haiti built it on a mountain top after winning its freedom, with hundreds of cannon, so that France could never take the country back.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The Citadelle Laferrière in northern Haiti about 1820, seen from the mountain path below its northern point: a fortress of gray stone on the very top of a peak, its walls about twenty times a man\'s height, coming to a sharp edge that points straight at us like the bow of a great ship, with rows of square gun ports along both sides. Cloud drifts below the walls, and the green mountains and the far sea lie lower still. On the path, three people of the Kingdom of Haiti, each different: a soldier in a dark uniform coat with white cross-belts and a musket, a mason with a hammer and a plumb line, and a woman with a load of provisions on her head. Stacks of iron cannonballs stand by the track. No palm-beach scenery, no ruins, no chains. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    orleans: { cap: 'The Loire, besieged 1428 to 1429', ll: [1.909, 47.902], fact: 'The city on the Loire was the key to the south of France, and in 1429 Joan of Arc\'s arrival ended an English siege of seven months in nine days.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'Orléans in the spring of 1429, during the English siege, seen from low on the River Loire, in the water just beside the southern end of the bridge, looking north along the line of the bridge to the city, as if standing at the end of the bridge: the bridge runs straight away from us, its two parapets converging toward the city gate in the very middle of the picture, with Les Tourelles in the right foreground at the near end of the bridge and the barge in the left foreground. The long stone bridge of many arches runs straight away from us across the wide, shallow river to the city gate on the far bank. At its near end, close to us and large in the picture, stands Les Tourelles, the English-held fort: two tall round stone towers with conical roofs, built on one arch of the bridge itself, with a bulwark of earth and timber on the near bank beside it. Just beyond the fort, one arch of the bridge has been broken down: there is a clear gap in the bridge with open water below it and the broken stone ends of the bridge on either side, with nothing across it; farther along there is a timber barricade on the arch where a tall stone cross stands; then the arches go on to the city\'s gatehouse. Along the far bank, the city wall with round towers, the roofs of the town and the long roof of the cathedral, which has no towers and no spire. Black smoke from the burned suburbs and white gun smoke drift across a stormy spring sky with a gleam of sun. On the river to the left of the bridge, a long, flat, wide Loire barge with a square bow and a single big square sail on a tall mast, the sail hung from a yard across the top of the mast and filled by the wind, loaded with sacks and barrels, comes on. Three people, each different: the boatman standing at the stern of the barge, steering it with both hands on the loom of one great steering oar that rests on the stern and trails out behind the barge, its broad flat wooden blade in the water behind the boat, like a rudder (he holds no pole and nothing touches the river bed); a woman sitting on the sacks in the barge, holding a sack in her arms and staring up at the English fort; and, small and far off, up on top of the city wall, on the walkway behind its battlements, a townsman beside a long iron gun lying on a plain wooden bed with no wheels, its muzzle poking out over the battlements toward the English fort, smoke puffing from it. No knight in armor, no banner in front. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    siracusa: { cap: 'Sicily, besieged 213 to 212 BC', ll: [15.293, 37.062], fact: 'When Rome attacked in 213 BC, the war machines Archimedes built kept its walls from being taken for about two years.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The fort of Euryalus, which guarded the landward end of the walls of Syracuse, in 213 BC, under a stormy evening sky, seen from high above and in front of it, as a bird would see it, looking down at an angle across the ditches to the fort, so that we look down into each ditch. Between us and the fort, exactly three separate ditches are cut one behind another across the flat pale rock (count them: one, two, three), each a deep straight trench sunk into the rock, its two sheer walls, the near one and the far one, and its flat floor in shadow all in view, with a strip of level rock at the same height between one ditch and the next, so that all three read clearly as three separate trenches cut down into the ground, not as steps or terraces: the nearest one at the bottom of the picture, the middle one, and the third, the widest and deepest, right at the foot of the towers: the towers rise straight from its far wall, and the arched tunnel mouths at the foot of the towers open into its floor. So from the bottom of the picture up: ditch, strip of rock, ditch, strip of rock, ditch, towers. Rising from the far side of the third ditch stands a row of exactly five massive square towers of pale squared stone, side by side: count them, one, two, three, four, five. Each tower is plainly its own block, standing a little forward of the next with a deep vertical shadow between each pair, all five the same height and each about six times a man\'s height, and the plain wall that runs off at either end is lower than the towers. Their tops are joined into one flat fighting platform with a low parapet. On the platform stand two torsion catapults, seen from above so that their shape is clear, each built like a giant crossbow lying on a stand: a long, straight, horizontal wooden beam, longer than two men, points out over the parapet toward us; at its front end a short square wooden frame holds two upright bundles of twisted rope, and from them two thick wooden arms reach out sideways, left and right, like the limbs of a bow, so that from above each machine has the shape of a crossbow; a heavy bowstring joins the tips of the two arms and is drawn back in a V along the beam to a winch with handles at the beam\'s rear end; the whole machine rests on a low, sturdy wooden trestle. Nothing on it stands upright higher than a man; it is long and low, not tall. The left catapult has just loosed: its two arms have swung forward, its bowstring is straight, and a stone ball flies out over the ditches. Three defenders, each different, small on the platform: an engineer in a plain tunic sighting along the farther catapult\'s beam, a soldier in a bronze helmet heaving at its winch, and a boy carrying a stone ball the size of his head. No sea, no ships, no great claw, no tube, no barrel, nothing like a cannon, no press, no screw, no upright frame. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    zimbabwe: { cap: 'Zimbabwe, at its height 1250 to 1450', ll: [30.933, -20.267], fact: 'Its kings ruled the plateau between the Zambezi and the Limpopo from within stone walls eleven meters high, built without mortar.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'Great Zimbabwe about 1350, inside the narrow passage of the Great Enclosure: two curving walls of flat granite blocks laid in even courses without mortar, the outer wall about six times a man\'s height and wide at the foot, so close together that two people can just pass. Ahead, where the passage opens, stands a solid round tower of the same stonework, tapering toward its top, about five times a man\'s height. Along the top of the outer wall runs a band of stones set in a zigzag. Three people in the passage, each different: an elder with a carved staff, a woman with a clay pot on her head, and a boy leading a calf. Beyond the tower, the thatched roofs of round clay houses. No mortar, no ruins, no carved birds on the walls. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    machu: { cap: 'Peru, c. 1450', ll: [-72.55, -13.16], tx0: 'Only the player whose side it is on can walk Figures in.', fact: 'The Inca built it on a ridge high in the Andes, without mortar, wheels or iron tools, where only a few guarded paths lead in.', sure: 3, art: 'Machu Picchu on its ridge above the clouds, stone terraces and houses, a sharp green peak behind, a llama on a terrace.' },
    nanmadol: { cap: 'Pohnpei, built about 1180 to 1600', ll: [158.335, 6.845], fact: 'The Saudeleur rulers of Pohnpei built it of basalt columns on nearly a hundred artificial islets in a lagoon, and made the island\'s chiefs live there under their eye.', sure: 2, bs: 2, art: 'Nan Madol on the reef of Pohnpei about 1400: a wall of dark basalt columns, each a natural six-sided stone log longer than a man, stacked crosswise in alternating layers like a log cabin and curving up at the corners, about four times a man\'s height, rising straight out of a canal of still green water. A second walled islet shows beyond it, with mangroves and breadfruit trees. A slender outrigger canoe glides down the canal. Three people in it, each different: a paddler at the bow, a paddler at the stern, and an old chief seated between them with a wreath of leaves on his head and a basket of offerings in his lap. Their clothing is of plant fiber. No carved statues, no stone faces, no ships. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    sigiriya: { cap: 'Sri Lanka, about 477 to 495', ll: [80.76, 7.957], fact: 'King Kashyapa built his palace on top of this rock, two hundred meters above the forest, and the way up passed between the paws of a great lion built of brick.', sure: 2, bs: 2, art: 'Sigiriya about 490, at the Lion Gate half way up the rock: a sheer wall of orange-streaked rock going up out of the picture, more than a hundred times a man\'s height in all, and against it the front of a huge crouching lion built of brick and white plaster, of which the two great paws rest on a terrace, each paw taller than a man, with a stairway climbing between them into the lion\'s chest. The head above is partly lost in the glare. Three people on the stair, each different: a guard with a spear at the foot, and two women of the court climbing, one with a tray of flowers, one holding a round fan on a long handle. Far below, the straight pools of the water gardens. No tourists\' iron stairs, no ruins, no elephants. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    // Culture
    rapanui: { cap: 'Easter Island, settled about 1200', ll: [-109.35, -27.12], fact: 'Polynesian voyagers settled this island about eight hundred years ago, and its clans carved nearly a thousand stone moai to honor their ancestors.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'Rapa Nui about 1500: three moai standing on a platform of fitted stone with their backs to the sea, seen from the grass below and in front, each different in height, the tallest about three times a man\'s height. Each is a long-eared, heavy-browed head and body of grey-brown volcanic stone with hands laid on the belly, a cylinder of red stone on its head, and eyes of white coral with dark pupils that look up and inland. On the paved slope before them, three islanders, each different: an old carver with a hafted stone adze over his shoulder, a woman in a bark-cloth cloak laying down a bunch of bananas, and a child. A few tall palms still stand behind. No row of many identical statues, no fallen statues, no ships. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    colosseum: { cap: 'Rome, opened AD 80', ll: [12.49, 41.89], tx0: 'Winning a clash here scores 1 Renown.', fact: 'Rome\'s emperors gave the people games here to keep their favor, and it could seat around 50,000.', sure: 3, art: 'The Colosseum in Rome seen from the arena floor looking up, tiers of roaring crowd, awnings and banners, sand and long shadows.' },
    olympia: { cap: 'The Peloponnese, games from 776 BC', ll: [21.63, 37.64], fact: 'Every four years the Greek cities called a truce so that athletes could travel here to compete, and the winners\' names were kept for more than a thousand years.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'Olympia about 450 BC, on the last day of the games, at the foot of the temple of Zeus: a wide flight of steps and a row of thick Doric columns of stone, plainly fluted, about six times a man\'s height, with plain cushion-shaped tops, and the beam above them painted in bands of blue and red, with old olive trees and two clothed bronze statues of past winners, small and to one side, on stone bases. Three people, each different: a judge of the games, an older man in a purple robe holding a forked rod, lifting a wreath of wild olive leaves; a young winner in a short tunic with a cloak over one shoulder, a wool ribbon tied round his head and a palm branch in his hand, bowing his head for it; and a boy holding a basket with more wreaths. Dust and heat. No stone stadium seats, no torch, no rings, no medals. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    prophetstown: { cap: 'Indiana, 1808 to 1811', ll: [-86.84, 40.5], fact: 'Tecumseh and his brother Tenskwatawa founded it in 1808, and people of many nations came to hear the Prophet and join the confederacy.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'Prophetstown in 1810, on a rise above the Wabash River in Indiana: an open town with no wall, fence or stockade round it, of houses framed with poles and covered with sheets of bark, some round-roofed and some long, with a large council house of logs at its middle, cooking fires, and fields of corn, beans and squash running down to the river. Three people arriving along the path, each different and each of a different nation: a Shawnee man in a calico hunting shirt and leggings, a cloth turban wrapped round his head and silver rings in his ears, carrying a long rifle; a Potawatomi woman in a wool blanket and a skirt edged with ribbon, with a pack on her back; and an old Kickapoo man with a staff. Canoes on the bank. No tipis, no feather war bonnets, no totem poles, no horses in a row. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    acropolis: { cap: 'Athens, 5th century BC', ll: [23.726, 37.971], tx0: 'It scores double Renown.', fact: 'Athens spent the tribute of its allies on the temples here, to show all Greece who led it, and the Parthenon was finished in 432 BC.', sure: 3, art: 'The Acropolis of Athens seen from below, the Parthenon bright on the rock above the city, a procession climbing the steps.' },
    globe: { cap: 'London, 1599', ll: [-0.097, 51.508], tx0: 'Arts Figures get +1 Might here.', fact: 'It held about 3,000 people, and all London, from apprentices to lords, came to its plays.', sure: 3, art: 'The Globe Theatre in London, a round timber playhouse with a thatched roof seen from the crowded yard, players on the stage, flags flying.' },
    wien: { cap: 'Austria, the city of music about 1780 to 1830', ll: [16.37, 48.21], fact: 'The emperor and the nobles of Vienna competed to keep the best musicians, and Haydn, Mozart, Beethoven and Schubert all worked here within fifty years.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'A music room in a nobleman\'s palace in Vienna about 1785, an hour before a concert: a high room with tall mirrors, a painted ceiling and a polished floor, lit by a servant who is lighting the candles one by one. In the middle stands a small piano of the time, a slim five-octave instrument with a wooden frame and thin legs, its lid up and its stool empty. Three people, each different: the footman in livery reaching up with a taper, a violinist in a powdered wig tuning with his ear close to the strings, and a horn player holding a natural horn, a plain coil of brass with no valves, warming the mouthpiece in his hand. Gilt chairs wait in an uneven half circle. No famous composer, no orchestra in rows, no grand piano with an iron frame. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    sistine: { cap: 'Rome, ceiling painted 1508 to 1512', ll: [12.4545, 41.9031], fact: 'The popes brought the best painters in Italy to this chapel to show the glory of Rome, and Michelangelo painted its ceiling between 1508 and 1512.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'The Sistine Chapel in 1513, the ceiling newly finished, seen from the floor near the entrance looking along its length: a tall, plain, barrel-vaulted hall, about twelve times a man\'s height, its vault covered with painted scenes and seated figures between painted stone ribs, shown only as soft color and shape. The side walls have a row of framed paintings with painted curtains below them. A marble screen crosses the floor, solid carved marble to waist height with a tall iron grille above it,, and a small balcony for the choir juts from the right wall. The far wall has two tall windows and an altarpiece, not the great painting that was added thirty years later. Three people, each different: a cardinal in red seen from behind, and two singers in the balcony sharing one huge choir book. No crowd, no scaffolding, no attempt to copy any one panel. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    taj: { cap: 'Agra, built about 1632 to 1653', ll: [78.042, 27.175], fact: 'Shah Jahan built it as a tomb for his wife Mumtaz Mahal, with craftsmen from across his empire, and it took about twenty years.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'The Taj Mahal about 1655, seen from inside the shadowed arch of its great gate, which frames it: at the end of a long water channel and a garden of fruit trees and flower beds stands the tomb of white marble on its wide platform, a great bulb-shaped dome about forty times a man\'s height from the ground, four smaller domed pavilions round it, and a slender, straight tower at each corner of the platform. Early light, the marble faintly pink. In the arch, three people, each different, seen from behind as dark shapes against the light: a gardener with a watering skin, a mother in a long veil, and a child pulling her forward by the hand. No tourists, no scaffolding, no boats. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    heian: { cap: 'Kyoto, the capital from 794', ll: [135.768, 35.011], fact: 'At its court a person rose by skill in poetry, music and handwriting, and Murasaki Shikibu wrote The Tale of Genji there about 1010.', sure: 2, bs: 2, art: 'A garden of a noble house in Heian-kyō about the year 1000, at a poetry party beside a winding stream: low wooden halls on posts with roofs of cypress bark, open verandas and rolled bamboo blinds stand behind a pond with a small arched bridge. A shallow stream curls across the moss, and a lacquer cup floats down it on a little raft. Three people seated apart along the bank, each different: a nobleman in a wide black court robe and a tall black lacquered cap, holding a brush over a strip of paper, frowning for a word; a lady in many layers of colored robes, her hair to the floor, hiding a laugh behind a fan; and an old musician with a lute across his knees. Plum blossom. No swords, no castle, no temple pagoda in front. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    deirelbahari: { cap: 'Thebes, about 1470 BC', ll: [32.607, 25.738], fact: 'Hatshepsut built her temple against the cliffs at Thebes about 1470 BC and had her trading voyage to the land of Punt carved on its walls.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'Hatshepsut\'s temple at Deir el-Bahari about 1465 BC, newly built, seen from its middle terrace looking along a colonnade of square white pillars toward the long ramp that climbs to the terrace above, with the sheer golden cliffs rising behind and above everything, several times the height of the whole temple. The walls behind the pillars are carved and painted with ships and trees. Beside the ramp, small trees grow in round pits cut into the rock. Three people, each different: a priest with a shaved head in a white linen robe, and two gardeners carrying a young incense tree between them, its roots in a basket slung from a pole on their shoulders. Hot, clean and bright. No queen in front, no ruins, no camels. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    versailles: { cap: 'France, the court from 1682', ll: [2.12, 48.805], fact: 'Louis XIV moved his court here in 1682 and kept the nobles of France busy with its plays, music and ceremony, with Molière and Lully at work for him.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'Versailles on a summer night about 1675: an opera given in the open air in the Marble Court, the small inner courtyard of the palace, paved in black and white marble, with walls of red brick and pale stone, gilded balconies, and orange trees in tubs. Hundreds of candles on stands and along the balconies light it. Three people, each different: a singer in a plumed helmet and a stiff, glittering costume with one arm flung out, in the middle of a song; a violinist in a long wig, playing; and the music master in front of the players, beating time on the floor with a tall staff. In the foreground stands the back of a single empty armchair, larger than the others. No fireworks, no hall of mirrors, no king\'s face. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    stonehenge: { cap: 'Salisbury Plain, c. 2500 BC', ll: [-1.83, 51.18], tx0: 'Whoever holds it gets +1 Spark next turn.', fact: 'People came from all over Britain to gather here at midwinter, and its builders hauled some of the stones about 150 miles from Wales.', sure: 3, art: 'Stonehenge at midsummer sunrise about 2500 BC, newly built and complete, the sun breaking between the standing stones, long shadows across the grass, a few small figures of its Neolithic builders in plain wool and hide clothing gathered to watch. No hooded robes, no druids.' },
    giza: { cap: 'Egypt, finished about 2560 BC', ll: [31.134, 29.979], fact: 'Khufu\'s builders raised it so that the king would be remembered as a god, and for about 3,800 years nothing people built stood taller.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'The Great Pyramid of Giza about 2560 BC, in the reign of Khufu, nearly finished and standing alone on its desert plateau above the green valley: four smooth, flat faces cased in fine white limestone from the Tura quarries across the river, the lit face blazing white in the low morning sun and the other in deep blue shadow; the top comes to a plain point of the same white stone, with no gold and no cap of another color. It is drawn true to its size, about eighty-five times a man\'s height: a ramp of rubble and mud brick still leans against one side, and the workers dragging a block up it are only specks, a man no taller than one course of its stone. Dawn: the low sun from the left throws long blue shadows, a hot wind lifts a veil of gold dust across the plateau, and a dark bank of cloud stands behind the pyramid so that its lit face blazes against it. In the foreground, on a canal harbor well in front of the pyramid and far from it, a broad wooden cargo boat of the time rides low in the water, heading to the right: a flat-bottomed hull of short planks with the stern curving up at the left end, a two-legged mast lowered along the deck, and three white casing blocks on board, each as long as a man and half as high. The boat is towed forward, bow first: a tow rope runs from the bow post at its right end forward to the bank, where the hauler walks ahead of the boat. Exactly three people, each different, and no one else on the boat: an inspector in a white kilt standing in the bow at the right end with a roll of papyrus, a steersman standing at the stern at the left end with a pair of long steering oars, one in each hand, like the two steering oars on the boats in Old Kingdom tomb pictures: one oar goes down over the near side of the stern and one over the far side, both slanting back into the water behind the boat, their two broad blades both in view in the water, and a hauler on the bank ahead of the boat, his back to it, leaning hard forward into the tow rope over his shoulder. The boat and its crew are small, low in the left of the picture. No other pyramids, no sphinx, no camels, no stepped sides, no gold. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    chichen: { cap: 'Yucatán, at its height 900 to 1100', ll: [-88.57, 20.683], fact: 'Its great ball court, 168 meters long, is the largest in ancient America, and the Maya city around it drew pilgrims to its sacred well for centuries.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'The great ball court of Chichén Itzá about the year 1000, seen from one end looking along it: a long alley of packed earth between two sheer stone walls about four and a half times a man\'s height and as long as a hundred men lying end to end, each with a stone ring set upright high in its middle, and a small temple on top. The walls and a low carved bench at their foot are plastered and painted in red, blue and yellow. Three players, each different, in padded belts of leather and wood, knee guards and feather headdresses: one sliding on his hip to strike a solid rubber ball, as big as a man\'s head, without using his hands, one leaping aside, one waiting near the ring. A stepped pyramid shows over the wall. No sacrifice, no skulls, no hoop like a basketball net. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    kumasi: { cap: 'Ghana, the Asante capital from about 1700', ll: [-1.62, 6.69], fact: 'The Golden Stool, which the Asante hold to be the soul of their nation, has been kept at Kumasi since about 1700, and in 1900 Yaa Asantewaa led a war to keep it from the British.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'Kumasi about 1817, as an English visitor of that year drew it: a broad, clean street of houses whose open-fronted rooms are raised on platforms of polished red clay, with whitewashed upper walls molded in raised patterns and steep thatched roofs, shaded by great trees. A chief\'s party passes under a huge state umbrella of bright cloth with a gold carving on its top. Three people of it, each different: an old spokesman in a cloth of woven strips worn over the left shoulder, carrying a staff with a gold top; a young man blowing a side-blown horn made of an elephant tusk; and the umbrella bearer, looking up as he turns the umbrella. The Golden Stool itself is not shown. No guns, no masks, no skulls. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    // Law
    tordesillas: { cap: 'Castile, 1494', ll: [-5, 41.5], fact: 'Here in 1494 Spain and Portugal drew a line down the Atlantic and agreed that newly found lands to the west of it were Spain\'s and those to the east Portugal\'s.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'Tordesillas in Castile, June 1494, seen from above and to one side, looking down on a heavy oak table in a whitewashed upper room: a sea chart on parchment is spread across it, with the coasts of Spain, Portugal and West Africa drawn at its right edge, a few islands far to the left, and empty sea between, crossed by fine compass lines. One hand holds a long ruler straight up and down the middle of the sea, from the chart\'s top edge to its bottom edge, and another hand draws one red line with a pen along the ruler\'s edge; there is only that one red line. Three people, each different, seen from behind and above, their faces not shown: an envoy of Castile in a dark velvet gown with a gold chain, an envoy of Portugal in a fur-edged gown, and a notary with ink-stained fingers. Two wax seals, a pair of dividers and a sand box lie by the chart. No globe, no map of America, no pope. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    runnymede: { cap: 'The Thames, June 1215', ll: [-0.565, 51.444], fact: 'In this meadow in 1215 the barons made King John put his seal to Magna Carta, which said that even the king must obey the law.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'Runnymede, a water meadow beside the River Thames, in June 1215: an open-sided tent of striped cloth pitched on the grass, and under it a trestle table with a single large sheet of parchment covered in close lines of writing. The king\'s folding chair stands empty behind the table. Three people, each different: a clerk in a plain gown pressing a lump of soft wax between the two halves of a seal press, to hang the king\'s great seal from the sheet by a cord; a baron in a long shirt of mail with a cloth surcoat over it, bareheaded, one hand on his sword hilt, watching the clerk\'s hands; and an archbishop in a mitre holding the corner of the sheet flat. Willows and the river behind. Nobody signs with a pen: the charter was sealed. No crown, no castle. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    aachen: { cap: 'The Rhineland, about 800', ll: [6.084, 50.775], fact: 'Charlemagne ruled most of western Europe from his palace here, and thirty-one kings were crowned in its chapel over the six hundred years after him.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'Charlemagne\'s palace chapel at Aachen about 805, seen from the floor of its eight-sided middle looking up: heavy piers carry round arches striped in light and dark stone, a gallery above them is screened with bronze railings and pairs of polished marble columns, and a dome of gold mosaic closes the top, about eighteen times a man\'s height from the floor. In the gallery, straight ahead and high up, stands a plain throne of white marble slabs at the top of six steps. It is empty. On the floor, three people, each different: a priest with a great book in both arms, and two Frankish courtiers in knee-length tunics and cloaks pinned at the shoulder, one old and one young, looking up at the throne. No pointed Gothic windows, no great hanging lamp, no crowned figure. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    forum: { cap: 'Rome, the center of the city from about 500 BC', ll: [12.4853, 41.8925], fact: 'Rome\'s Senate met beside it and its courts sat in it, and the laws made here were obeyed from Britain to Egypt.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The Roman Forum about AD 10, whole and in use, seen from the paving at its western end: a speakers\' platform of stone with the bronze rams of captured warships fixed point outward along its front: each a heavy bronze ship\'s beak, a blunt pointed casting with three flat horizontal fins across its tip, not an animal\'s head and nothing with horns, the tall plain Senate house with bronze doors to one side, and beyond them temples with columns about six times a man\'s height, their roofs tiled and their carving painted. Three people, each different: a senator in a white toga over a tunic with a broad purple stripe, standing on the platform with one arm out, in the middle of a speech; an attendant below him holding a bundle of rods tied with red straps; and a clerk on the steps writing on a wax tablet. Pigeons, long light. No ruins, no broken columns, no arena, no crowd in rows. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    independence: { cap: 'Philadelphia, 1776 and 1787', ll: [-75.15, 39.949], fact: 'The Declaration of Independence was signed in this hall in Philadelphia in 1776 and the Constitution in 1787, and Benjamin Franklin signed both.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'The Assembly Room of the Pennsylvania State House in Philadelphia early on a July morning in 1776, before the delegates arrive: a plain, handsome panelled room painted gray, with tall windows, a row of small tables covered in green cloth, each with a candlestick, quill pens and papers, and wooden Windsor chairs pushed back as they were left. On a low platform at the end stands the president\'s table with a silver inkstand. Three people, each different: an old doorkeeper folding back a shutter so that a bar of sunlight falls across the tables, a young clerk laying out a large sheet of parchment, and one early delegate in the doorway with his hat under his arm, mopping his face. No painted sun on the chair, no crowd, no flag, no bell. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    persepolis: { cap: 'Persia, begun about 518 BC', ll: [52.891, 29.935], fact: 'The kings of Persia received the tribute of their empire here, and its stairways are carved with the envoys of twenty-three peoples bringing gifts.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'Persepolis about 480 BC, at the foot of the great stair to the audience hall: a broad double stairway of gray stone whose wall is carved with a procession of gift-bearers, and above it a forest of slender fluted columns about eleven times a man\'s height, each topped with a pair of kneeling bulls, carrying a cedar roof, all freshly painted. Three living people climb the stair beside their carved likenesses, each different: a court usher in a long pleated robe and a tall fluted hat leading the way with a staff; an envoy from Nubia with an elephant tusk on his shoulder; and an envoy of the Saka in a pointed cap, trousers and a short coat, carrying gold bracelets on a tray. No ruins, no fallen columns, no fire. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    babylon: { cap: 'Mesopotamia, Hammurabi\'s city about 1754 BC', ll: [44.42, 32.542], fact: 'Hammurabi ruled from this city, and about 1754 BC he had his 282 laws carved on stone and set up where anyone could see them.', sure: 2, bs: 1, art: 'A temple courtyard in Babylon about 1750 BC. Little of the city of that time has been found, so the setting is plain: high walls of mud brick with tall niches, a date palm, hard sunlight. In the middle stands the law stone: an upright pillar of polished black stone with a rounded top, about a third taller than a man, with a carving at its top of a standing king before a seated god who holds out a rod and a ring, and below it band after band of tiny wedge-shaped writing. Three people before it, each different: a scribe with a shaved head tracing a line of the writing with a reed and reading it aloud, a farmer twisting his cap in his hands, and an old widow leaning on a stick, listening. No blue glazed gate, no hanging gardens, no king. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    forbidden: { cap: 'Beijing, finished 1420', ll: [116.397, 39.917], fact: 'For five hundred years China was governed from behind its walls, and no one entered without the emperor\'s leave.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'The Forbidden City about 1500, under the Ming: the great throne hall seen across its vast paved courtyard from low down, standing on three stacked terraces of white marble with carved railings. The hall, with its terraces, is about twenty times a man\'s height: red columns and walls under a double roof of yellow glazed tiles with upturned corners. The courtyard is almost empty, which is the point. Three small people, each different: an official, seen from the side in profile so that his cap shows clearly, in a dark blue round-collared robe with a square badge on the chest and a black gauze cap with no brim and two long, flat, stiff wings sticking straight out sideways from its back, one to the left and one to the right, like two long flat paddles; the cap has no brim at all and is nothing like a round hat, walking toward the hall with a memorial in both hands, and two guards far apart at the foot of the stairs. Long morning shadows. No crowd, no dragons in the sky, no tourists, no name board or writing on the hall. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    onondaga: { cap: 'New York, the council fire of the Haudenosaunee', ll: [-76.15, 42.99], fact: 'By Haudenosaunee tradition the Great Law of Peace was agreed here, and the council of the nations has kept its fire at Onondaga ever since.', sure: 1, bs: 2, art: 'Onondaga long before Europeans came, as Haudenosaunee tradition tells of the founding of the Great Law of Peace: a clearing above a lake, with a long house framed with bent poles and covered with sheets of elm bark, as long as twelve men lying end to end, smoke rising through holes along its roof. In front of it grows a great white pine, and a small council fire burns at its foot. Three people at the fire, each different: a speaker standing with strings of shell beads, some white and some dark purple, hanging from his raised hand, a clan mother seated with a rattle in her lap made from a whole turtle\'s shell, its domed patterned shell and the turtle\'s stretched neck forming the handle (not a drum), and an old chief listening with his eyes closed. Their clothes are of deerskin, with shell and bone ornaments. No horses, no cloth, no guns, no tipis, no feather war bonnets. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    constantinople: { cap: 'The Bosporus, the capital from 330', ll: [28.98, 41.008], fact: 'Emperors ruled from this city for more than eleven hundred years, and Justinian\'s lawyers here wrote the code that much of Europe\'s law grew from.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'Constantinople about 550, under Justinian, seen from the paved square in front of the great church of Holy Wisdom: a vast building of brick and stone crowned by one wide, low dome on a ring of windows, about thirty times a man\'s height, with half-domes stepping down from it. In the square stands a tall column of stone drums with a bronze horseman on its top. Three people cross the square, each different: a lawyer in a long white tunic and a cloak pinned at the shoulder with a codex of laws under his arm, a palace guard with a round shield and a spear, and a water seller with a clay jar on his back. The sea shows between the buildings. No minarets, no crescent, no ruins. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    nineveh: { cap: 'Assyria, 7th century BC', ll: [43.15, 36.36], tx0: 'A quiet Place with no power of its own.', fact: 'The kings of Assyria ruled an empire that reached from the Nile to Iran from this city, and Ashurbanipal\'s library here held tens of thousands of clay tablets.', sure: 3, art: 'A gate of Nineveh, capital of Assyria, about 690 BC, seen straight on from directly in front of it, close and low, in strict one-point perspective looking down the gate passage like a corridor. The passage is a deep tunnel with a round semicircular arch through a massive mud-brick city wall with stepped crenellations, and at its far end, at the vanishing point, the sunlit city shows. The two side walls of this corridor ARE a matched pair of colossal lamassu, human-headed winged bulls about four and a half meters tall (two and a half times a man\'s height), each carved from a single block of pale gypsum stone and built into the gateway as its door jambs. At the mouth of the passage, at its left corner and its right corner, the front of each bull faces straight out at us: a calm bearded human face, the tall rounded crown of a god with three pairs of horns curving up its front and a ring of feathers and a band of rosettes at the top, hair in curls to the shoulders, bull\'s ears with earrings, a long squared beard in tiers of curls, a broad chest covered in rows of curls, and two forelegs planted together. Behind each front, its long body runs straight back away from us down the inside of the passage, lining the corridor wall and steeply foreshortened: the feathered wing swept back, the flank, the striding hind legs and the wedge-shaped writing carved between the legs all recede toward the vanishing point, the left bull\'s flank forming the left wall of the corridor and the right bull\'s flank forming the right wall, mirror images of each other. The outside face of the city wall to the left and right of the gateway is completely plain brick: no statue, wing or relief on it. People give the true scale: each bull is about two and a half times the height of a man, so a robed man leading a laden donkey out through the passage, walking toward us between the two bulls in the foreground, stands as tall as the bulls\' chests, his head level with the bottom of their beards, and a spearman on guard stands beside one bull\'s foreleg at the same size. The passage between the bulls is wide enough for a loaded cart. The round arch springs from just above the bulls\' crowns. Their ears are a bull\'s ears, set at the sides of the head below the crown, and the horns lie flat against the front of the crown, curving up it, not sticking out. Low evening sun lights the two stone faces, with deep shadow inside the passage. Symmetrical. Not a pointed arch.' },
    winchester: { cap: 'Wessex, Alfred\'s capital from 871', ll: [-1.31, 51.063], fact: 'Alfred the Great ruled Wessex from here, and the code of laws he drew up about 890 bound his kingdom together against the Danes.', sure: 2, bs: 2, art: 'Winchester about 890, in the reign of Alfred: a court of law held in the open air before a timber hall with a steep thatched roof and carved door posts, inside the patched Roman wall of the town. Three people, each different: a royal reeve, a grey-bearded man in a knee-length tunic and a cloak pinned with a round brooch, holding a small book of laws open in both hands; and two freemen before him, a tall angry one and a short stubborn one, each with his right hand laid on a gospel book that rests on a folding stool, swearing. A spear is stuck upright in the ground. Behind the hall, a small stone church with a plain square tower. No castle, no horned helmets, no crown, no great cathedral. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    // Routes
    silk: { cap: 'Samarkand, Central Asia', ll: [66.97, 39.65], tx0: 'It is linked to every Place.', fact: 'It was a web of caravan routes linking China and the Mediterranean, and the cities along it grew rich and powerful on what passed through.', sure: 3, art: 'A caravan city on the Silk Road: a crowded market under striped awnings, bolts of silk and spice sacks, camels arriving through a tiled gate, mountains beyond.' },
    cape: { cap: 'Southern Africa, rounded in 1488', ll: [18.47, -34.36], fact: 'Bartolomeu Dias rounded it in 1488, and the sea road it opened between Europe and Asia carried the spice trade until the Suez Canal opened in 1869.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The Cape of Good Hope about 1500, seen from the sea in heavy weather: a headland of gray sandstone cliffs rising straight out of the surf, about ten times as tall as the mainmast of the one small ship below it, with low green scrub on its back and no building of any kind. A Portuguese caravel, a slim ship about fifteen paces long with two masts and slanting triangular sails, heels hard as it beats round the point, spray breaking over its bow, a plain red cross on the larger sail. Only three sailors can be seen on its deck: one at the tiller, one hauling a rope, one clinging to the rail and pointing at the land. Albatrosses ride the wind. No lighthouse, no road, no town. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    lanse: { cap: 'Newfoundland, about 1021', ll: [-55.53, 51.6], fact: 'Norse sailors built a base here to mend their ships and explore farther south, and wood they cut has been dated to the year 1021.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'The Norse base at L\'Anse aux Meadows in northern Newfoundland about the year 1021, as the excavations found it: three long, low halls with walls of stacked turf as thick as a man is tall and roofs of turf on timber, the walls about a man\'s height and the ridges about twice that, on a grassy terrace above a shallow gray bay, with a small hut for smelting bog iron beside a brook. On the beach a broad, clinker-built cargo ship lies hauled out on rollers with one plank off. Three people, each different: a broad smith with singed sleeves hammering a red-hot rivet on a stone anvil, a woman in a long wool dress with a pair of oval brooches spinning thread on a hand spindle, and a gray-haired shipwright trimming the new plank with a broad axe. Cold, clear light, low bog and stunted spruce. No horned helmets, no stone houses, no dragon heads. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    dunhuang: { cap: 'Gansu, a garrison from 111 BC', ll: [94.66, 40.14], fact: 'The northern and southern branches of the Silk Road met at this oasis, so every caravan between China and the West passed through it.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The Jade Gate pass near Dunhuang about AD 100, where the Silk Road left China: a square fort of rammed yellow earth with walls about six times a man\'s height and one arched gate, alone on a gravel plain, with a beacon tower of earth and reed bundles beyond it and great sand dunes on the horizon. A caravan of two-humped Bactrian camels kneels to be unloaded at the gate. Three people, each different: a bearded caravan leader from the western cities in a belted knee-length coat and a tall felt cap, a Chinese frontier clerk in a dark robe and a small cap reading a narrow wooden tally slip, and a boy holding the lead camel\'s nose rope. Low evening sun, long shadows. No market stalls, no tiled roofs, no horses with stirrups. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    magellanstrait: { cap: 'Patagonia, found in 1520', ll: [-70.9, -53.5], fact: 'Magellan\'s ships found this passage between the Atlantic and the Pacific in 1520, and for nearly a hundred years it was the only known way through.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The Strait of Magellan in November 1520: a channel of dark, wind-whipped water between steep mountains streaked with snow, a blue-white glacier coming down to the sea on one side. Two small ships with high sterns and square sails, each about twenty paces long and dwarfed by the mountains, feel their way up the channel one behind the other. On the southern shore, low down and far off, three small fires burn in the dusk with thin smoke rising, the fires that gave the land its name. In front of the ships a rowing boat takes soundings: three sailors, each different, one heaving a lead line, one at the oars, one in the bow looking for rocks. Cold, grand and empty. No town, no lighthouse. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    appian: { cap: 'Rome to Brindisi, begun 312 BC', ll: [12.52, 41.84], fact: 'Rome built it in 312 BC to move its legions south quickly, the first of the roads that held the empire together.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'The Appian Way a few miles south of Rome about AD 50, seen from the middle of the road at knee height: a road paved with large, many-sided blocks of dark gray stone fitted like a puzzle, a little wider than two men lying end to end, with a raised kerb on each side, running dead straight to the horizon between umbrella pines, cypress trees and stone tombs. A round stone milestone stands at the roadside. Three people, each different: a legionary on the march with his kit slung from a forked pole over his shoulder, an imperial courier driving a light two-wheeled cart with a pair of mules, and a road mender on his knees resetting a block with an iron bar. Ruts worn by wheels show in the stone. No marching column, no chariots racing, no ruins. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    grandcanal: { cap: 'Eastern China, joined up in 609', ll: [119.4, 32.4], fact: 'Finished in 609, it joined the rice lands of the south to the capitals of the north, about 1,800 kilometers, and fed China\'s governments for thirteen centuries.', sure: 3, bs: 2, art: 'The Grand Canal about 750, in Tang China: a straight, wide waterway between raised earth banks planted with willows, crossing flat rice country. A long, flat-bottomed grain barge with a mat awning and a tall steering oar is being towed against the current by a rope from the top of its mast. Three haulers on the towpath, each different, lean into the rope with wooden boards across their chests: a wiry old man in front, a broad young one, and a boy at the back. On the barge a clerk in a robe and a black cap counts the sacks and writes the number with a brush on a strip of paper, beside the helmsman. A second barge is far ahead, small. No stone-arch city bridges, no steamboats, no pagoda in front. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    qhapaq: { cap: 'The Andes, the Inca road about 1450', ll: [-72, -13.5], fact: 'The Inca tied their empire together with some 40,000 kilometers of road, and relay runners carried the ruler\'s orders along it about 240 kilometers a day.', sure: 2, bs: 2, art: 'The Inca road about 1500, climbing a mountainside in the high Andes: a stairway of fitted stone about as wide as two men lying end to end, with a low wall on the outer edge and a stone-lined drain crossing it, zigzagging up toward a pass, with a snow peak far behind. Beside the road stands a small stone hut with a thatched roof, a relay post. Three people, each different: a runner arriving at full stretch, holding a trumpet made of a large sea shell to his lips with one hand to warn the post and holding out a bundle of knotted colored cords with the other; a second runner, fresh, already moving away up the stair, reaching back to take the cords from the first runner\'s hand; and, apart from them, a herder with two loaded llamas pressed against the wall to let them by, holding nothing out. No wheels, no horses, no rope bridge. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    basra: { cap: 'Iraq, founded 636', ll: [47.78, 30.51], fact: 'Ships sailed from this port down the Gulf to India and China in the days of the caliphs of Baghdad, and brought back the silk and spices their court was known for.', sure: 2, bs: 2, art: 'The waterside at Basra about 850, in the time of the caliphs of Baghdad: a broad, slow river lined with date palms, and on its bank a seagoing ship of the Gulf hauled half out of the water for repair, a deep hull about twenty paces long whose planks are sewn edge to edge with cord and have no nails at all. Three people, each different: a shipwright sitting on a plank slung over the side, pulling a stitch of palm-fibre cord tight with both hands and a foot; a ship\'s master in a long robe and a turban checking a row of big green-glazed storage jars from China that stand on the bank; and a boy tarring the hull with a brush of palm leaf. Another ship under one great slanting sail goes down river. No minarets in front, no cannon, no steering wheel. This is a wide picture of a place: the place itself fills the middle of the picture and can still be read when the picture is shown small; the three people are smaller than the place and stand low down or to one side, never in front of the middle of the place, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
    kittyhawk: { cap: 'North Carolina, 17 December 1903', ll: [-75.67, 36.02], fact: 'On these dunes in 1903 the Wright brothers made the first powered flights, the longest of them 59 seconds.', sure: 3, bs: 3, art: 'Kill Devil Hills, near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, at half past ten on the morning of 17 December 1903, the moment of the first powered flight: a cold, hard north wind under a low gray winter sky, thin ice on puddles in the sand, sand and tufts of sea grass streaming in the wind. Seen from behind the machine and a little above it, from the slope of a dune, three-quarters on: the wings stretch across the middle of the picture, the machine flies away from us toward the upper left, and its right wing tip is the nearer one, at the right of the picture. The Flyer has just lifted off the far end of its launching rail and is about two feet above the sand, flying into the wind. Nearest to us, behind the middle of the wings, stands the twin rudder; just behind the back edge of the wings the two propellers spin; beyond the wings, out ahead of their middle, the elevator shows plainly as two flat surfaces one above the other, both of them in view. Because we look down from above and behind, the elevator, which is farther from us, shows just beyond the front edge of the upper wing; it is carried forward at the same height as the wings on long, thin horizontal rods that run forward from the front of the wings and skids, and nothing stands up from the top of the upper wing: no posts, no mast, no tower. Where each part is: the elevator and the rudder are both on the center line of the machine, the elevator out in front of the middle of the wings, ahead of the pilot\'s head, and the rudder out behind the middle of the wings, behind his feet. The two propellers are just behind the back edge of the wings, one on each side of the middle, a quarter of the way out toward each wing tip. Nothing at all is fixed to the ends of the wings. Draw the machine exactly as it was built, part by part: two long, flat, narrow wings of pale unbleached cloth over wooden ribs, one above the other, each as long as seven men lying end to end and about a man\'s height from front edge to back edge, held a man\'s height apart by many slim upright wooden struts and crossed wires; out in front of the wings, carried forward on long thin horizontal wooden rods at the height of the wings, the elevator: two smaller flat cloth surfaces, one above the other, about a third as wide as the wings; behind the back edge of the wings, between the upper and the lower wing, two separate wooden propellers, each a slim two-bladed propeller as wide across as a man and a half is tall, one to the left of the middle and one to the right, each on its own shaft, turning in opposite directions, driven by two long bicycle-type chains that run back to them from the engine; farther back, carried on a light frame of thin wooden rods, the rudder: two tall, narrow, upright cloth surfaces standing side by side a little apart, like two doors standing parallel, each taller than a man; underneath, two long wooden skids like sled runners that curve up at the front; no wheels. On the lower wing, just left of the middle, Orville Wright lies flat on his stomach, face forward, in a dark suit; just right of the middle, beside him on the lower wing, sits the small engine with an upright radiator. The launching rail is a single straight wooden beam, as long as ten men lying end to end, laid flat on the level sand and running straight away from us in the direction the machine flies; the machine has just passed over its far end, and the small wooden trolley it ran on is left behind on the rail under the machine\'s tail. Wilbur Wright, a lean man in a dark suit, a white collar and a dark cap, has just let go of the right wing tip, the wing tip nearest us, and is still running beside it on the sand, his coat flapping. Off to the right, a big man from the lifesaving station in a heavy coat stands behind a box camera on a tripod, squeezing the bulb. Behind: two weathered wooden sheds, low dunes and the great bare sand hill of Kill Devil Hill, a gray sky with torn, racing cloud. No wheels, no seat, no pilot sitting up, no crowd, no propeller at the front, no single tail fin, no more than two propellers. This is a wide picture of a place and its moment: the Flyer and the camp fill the middle of the picture, the machine large enough that each of its parts can be counted, and nothing that matters is at the far left or far right edge.' },
  };
  // (Domains whose names take "the" in the middle of a sentence: "Legacy Line from the Royal Society")
  var THE = {};
  'silk cape magellanstrait library greenwich longjiang gibraltar wall laferriere laurion colosseum appian padua wisdom acropolis globe sistine forum royalsociety forbidden taj tlatelolco grandcanal giza qhapaq'.split(' ').forEach(function (k) { THE[k] = 1; });
  // How one thing led to another, for every link the cards carry ('from>to'; a Place is '@id'): a Figure's Legacy
