  // The cards that have left the set (cards.js RETIRED), with the words they had: a game saved with one in it still
  // shows it, and so does the history of an old game. Nothing else reads these.
  var RETIRED = {
    scheherazade: { cap: 'The Thousand and One Nights', ll: [44.36, 33.31], tx: 'She can\'t be clashed.', fact: 'In the old tale she saves her life by telling the king a story each night for 1,001 nights.', art: 'Shahrazad (Scheherazade), the learned vizier\'s daughter of the Thousand and One Nights, telling a tale by night, composed like a medieval Persian manuscript painting: a poised young woman in modest, finely woven court dress with long sleeves and a light headscarf, seated on a carpet in the lower part of the picture with open books beside her and her younger sister Dunyazad listening, one hand raised in mid-story. From her words a great swirling cloud of the tales themselves rises and fills the upper two thirds of the picture: a merchant ship in a storm, the flying ebony horse with its rider, and a towering djinn drawn the way djinn appear in medieval Arabic and Persian manuscripts such as the Book of Wonders: a horned, fanged, fierce-faced spirit of smokeless fire with clawed hands, a tail, and flame curling from its body. The cloud rises from her storytelling and the incense smoke of a small brazier, not from a lamp. Dignified, intelligent, fully and modestly clothed. No magic lamp, no muscular blue genie, no turbaned cartoon genie, no dancer costume, no bare shoulders.' },
    anansi: { cap: 'West African legend', ll: [-1.6, 6.7], tx: 'When you play him, you gain 1 Spark, or 2 Spark if the other player holds this Place.', fact: 'In Akan stories, the spider Anansi wins all the world\'s stories from the sky god by his wits.', art: 'Kwaku Anansi, the spider of the Akan stories of Ghana, drawn as a real spider with no human features at all: a huge orb-weaving spider seen dramatically from below, sitting at the very hub of a great orb web strung between giant forest trees. The web is built as a real web is built: straight spokes all radiating out from the hub where the spider sits, and spiral threads circling around it. The spider is drawn clearly and symmetrically like a diagram: exactly eight long legs and no other limbs of any kind, four legs on his left and four legs on his right, all eight fully visible from body to tip, spread wide apart like the spokes of a wheel with clear space between each, each foot resting on a thread of the web. None of his legs is curled, folded or hidden, and he has no short front arms, feelers or pedipalps. He is not holding anything. The small carved wooden box of all the world\'s stories hangs directly beneath his head, wrapped in bands of white silk and tied to the hub of the web by silk threads, as his prize. His body is tilted slightly to one side and his cluster of eight round black eyes catches the light like beads as it stares straight down at us, sly, watchful and pleased with himself. The markings on his abdomen fall in bold bands and diamonds, like the geometric patterns of Asante weaving. Green forest light breaks through the canopy behind him, with heavy black shadows. A true spider: no face, no mask, no human head, hands, hair or clothing; not gory. Compose it so the spider\'s body, eyes and the box are in the upper half of the picture and the top left corner is plain canopy.' },
    atalanta: { cap: 'Arcadia, Greek legend', ll: [22.3, 37.5], tx: 'Swift. She has +2 Might when she attacks a stronger Figure.', fact: 'In Greek myth she could outrun anyone and joined the hunt for the Calydonian boar.', art: 'Atalanta, a lean huntress in a short tunic, sprinting flat out through a forest with a bow in hand and hair flying, a great boar crashing through brush ahead.' },
    daedalus: { cap: 'Crete, Greek legend', ll: [25.16, 35.3], tx: 'Voyager: he can walk to any Place.', fact: 'In Greek myth he built the Labyrinth, then escaped Crete on wings of feathers and wax.', art: 'Daedalus, the master craftsman of Greek myth, at the moment before his escape from Crete: a wiry, grey-bearded craftsman in a short workman\'s tunic, standing at a cliff edge with both arms spread wide, wearing the wings he built: a great wing of real bird feathers bound with cord and wax strapped along each arm from shoulder to fingertip, like a bird about to launch into the wind. Far below him lies the vast stone Labyrinth and the sea of Bronze Age Crete. The wings are clearly worn on his two arms, one each side, not carried on his shoulder, and he holds nothing else.' },
    odysseus: { cap: 'Ithaca, Greek legend', ll: [20.7, 38.4], tx: 'Voyager. Steadfast: he has +2 Might when he defends.', fact: 'In Homer\'s Odyssey it takes him ten years to sail home from Troy.', art: 'Odysseus, weathered and cunning, gripping the steering oar of a small ship in heavy seas, a great bow slung on his back, a rocky island ahead.' },
    beowulf: { cap: 'Geatland, Old English legend', ll: [13.5, 58.0], tx: 'Guard. Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'The poem about him, over 3,000 lines long, survives in a single manuscript about a thousand years old.', art: 'Beowulf, a huge bearded warrior in a mail shirt and boar-crested helmet, standing guard in the doorway of a great timber hall at night, firelight behind, a monstrous shadow outside.' },
    trojan: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Play a Figure that costs 3 or less from your hand at any Place, without paying for it.', fact: 'In the legend, Greek soldiers hid inside a giant wooden horse to get into Troy.', art: 'A giant wooden horse on wheels standing before open city gates at night, a hatch in its belly ajar with a rope dropping down.' },
    sinbad: { cap: 'The Thousand and One Nights', ll: [47.78, 30.5], tx: 'Voyager. When you play him, you gain 1 Spark.', fact: 'In the tales he sails from Basra seven times, meets whales as big as islands and birds that carry off elephants, and comes home richer every time.', art: 'Sinbad the Sailor on his second voyage, in the tale from the Thousand and One Nights, escaping a desert island by tying himself to the leg of the Rukh, the giant bird: the picture is filled by the enormous bird in flight, seen from below and to one side, its wings spread across the whole upper half, each feather the length of a ship\'s oar, one huge scaled leg trailing. Lashed to that leg with the long unwound cloth of his own turban is Sinbad, a merchant of Abbasid Baghdad of about thirty with a short dark beard, bare-headed, in a long belted tunic and loose trousers, both arms wrapped around the leg, looking down wide-eyed. Far below are the sea, the small island with the white dome of the bird\'s egg on it, and his ship sailing away without him. Composed like a medieval Arabic manuscript painting of the Book of Wonders, with flat bright color and patterned clouds. Sinbad and the leg are in the upper half, right of center. A merchant of the ninth century: no bare chest and waistcoat, no curled slippers, no scimitar, no flying carpet, no genie. Keep the top left corner wing feathers and sky.' },
    gilgamesh: { cap: 'Uruk, Mesopotamian legend', ll: [45.64, 31.32], tx: 'Guard: enemies here must clash with him first.', fact: 'A real king of Uruk, some 4,700 years ago, probably stands behind the legend. The poem about him, written on clay tablets, is one of the oldest stories we have.', art: 'Gilgamesh, king of Uruk in the old Mesopotamian epic, standing on the top of the city wall he built, at sunrise, as the poem\'s first and last lines invite us to see it: a huge, broad-shouldered man drawn after the Sumerian statues of his own age, with long black hair parted in the middle and falling to his shoulders, a long squared beard cut in ridges, heavy brows and very wide eyes, bare to the waist, wearing a long skirt of tufted wool in rows, a plain band around his brow, and in one hand, resting on his shoulder, a copper axe of his age: a slender straight wooden haft with a small, narrow copper blade socketed near its end, a tool no bigger than a hatchet. He stands feet apart on the broad walkway of the wall in the right half of the picture, his free hand laid flat on the brickwork of the parapet. The wall of baked brick runs away from him in a long curve to the left with its buttresses and a gate, and inside it lie the flat-roofed city, date-palm gardens, clay pits and a white temple on a high terrace. Seen from a little below on the walkway, from the knees up. His head is a little right of the center of the picture with a wide band of dawn sky above it: the top of his head is a fifth of the way down the picture, and the top right corner is empty sky. Early Sumer: no crown, no horned helmet, no winged bulls, no Assyrian royal dress, no lion, no writing, no broad double-curved battle axe. Keep the top left corner dawn sky.' },
    // (2026-11-13: held for a future Legends of Lore set)
    tomoe: { cap: 'Japan, c. 1157 to 1247', ll: [137.9, 36.2], tx: 'Swift: can walk on the turn it\'s played.', fact: 'The Tale of the Heike calls her a warrior worth a thousand, who rode into battle for Lord Kiso.', art: 'Tomoe Gozen, a samurai woman in laced armor with long hair flying, galloping on horseback with a curved naginata blade swung back, arrows in the air.' },
    hiawatha: { cap: 'Haudenosaunee tradition', ll: [-76.2, 43.07], tx: 'Law: enemy Figures here can\'t clash. Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'In the tradition of the Haudenosaunee he and the Peacemaker brought five warring nations to bury their weapons and join under the Great Law of Peace.', art: 'Hiawatha (Ayenwatha) of the Haudenosaunee, as the tradition of the Great Law of Peace tells of him, long before Europeans came: a man in his forties with a grave, gentle face, grieving, by a small fire at the edge of the forest beside a lake at sunrise, threading small white and purple shells from the lake onto strings and hanging the finished strings over a pole laid across two upright forked sticks: the first strings of wampum, made to carry words of condolence and peace. He wears deerskin leggings, a breechcloth and moccasins worked with dyed porcupine quills, and a deerskin robe over one shoulder; he is bare-headed, his long black hair tied back. He kneels on one knee, lifting a finished string of shells up to the light with both hands so that it hangs in front of his chest; two more strings already hang from the pole beside him. Behind him a tall white pine stands alone on the shore, and far across the water is one long bark-covered house with a rounded roof, with a single thin thread of smoke rising from the smoke hole in the middle of its roof. That thread and his own small fire are the only smoke in the picture: no row of smoke columns along the shore or the hills. The sun is low on the far shore, well below the top of the picture. Seen from the waist up, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the lifted string beside it in the upper half. Eastern Woodlands before European contact: no feather headdress, no feathers, no tipi, no horse, no glass beads or woven cloth, no tomahawk, nothing from Longfellow\'s poem. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    // (2026-11-12: these seven became Guides in the roster of twelve)
    joan: { cap: 'Orléans, c. 1412 to 1431', ll: [1.9, 47.9], tx: 'She has +2 Might while she is outnumbered here.', fact: 'At seventeen she led the army that lifted the siege of Orléans in 1429.', art: 'Joan of Arc, a fierce teenage girl in battered plate armor with cropped hair, planting a tall banner into the ground, castle towers behind her, wind and smoke streaming sideways.' },
    saladin: { cap: 'Damascus, c. 1137 to 1193', ll: [36.29, 33.51], tx: 'Steadfast: he has +2 Might when he defends.', fact: 'He retook Jerusalem in 1187 and was admired even by his enemies for keeping his word.', art: 'Saladin, the Ayyubid sultan, in 1187: calm and upright on a horse in a turban-wrapped helmet and a mail coat, a straight double-edged sword of the twelfth century lowered at his side, the walled city of Jerusalem on its hill behind him. A composed, dignified commander known for keeping his word. Historically accurate twelfth-century arms: a straight sword, not a curved scimitar.' },
    shakespeare: { cap: 'London, 1564 to 1616', ll: [-0.097, 51.508], tx: 'At the end of a round, if you hold his Place, gain 1 Renown.', fact: 'He wrote about 37 plays and added hundreds of words and phrases to English.', art: 'William Shakespeare with a high forehead, small beard and an earring, striding across a wooden stage with a quill behind his ear and pages in hand, a round open-air theater crowd below.' },
    mansamusa: { cap: 'Mali Empire, ruled c. 1312 to 1337', ll: [-8.4, 11.4], tx: 'When you play him, you gain 2 Spark.', fact: 'On his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324 he gave away so much gold that its price fell in Cairo for years.', art: 'Mansa Musa, ruler of the Mali Empire, on his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324: a dignified West African king on horseback in wide flowing embroidered robes, a wrapped white turban over a small gold cap, a golden staff in one hand and, in the other, a gold nugget the size of a hen\'s egg held out in front of his chest on his open palm, offered to the viewer, calm, devout and generous. Riding with him are only three people of his train, each one particular: a grey-bearded scholar on a mule holding a book to his chest, a young herald on foot in a striped robe carrying a staff topped with gold, and a veiled desert guide on a tall white camel pointing the way ahead. Behind them a few loaded camels rest in a loose group beside a well and two palms, and beyond is only empty dunes. His head is right of center, about a fifth of the way down, with nothing but plain sky in both top corners of the picture: no hand, staff head, palm or pointing arm reaches into a top corner (the plates of the card sit there). Historically grounded dress of a Sahelian Muslim ruler: no European crown, no spiked crown.' },
    sorjuana: { cap: 'Mexico City, c. 1648 to 1695', ll: [-99.13, 19.43], tx: 'Scholar: you draw a card when you play her.', fact: 'A nun in Mexico City, she filled her rooms with books and instruments and wrote in defense of a woman\'s right to study. Her first book called her the Tenth Muse.', art: 'Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz about 1690, in her library in the convent of San Jerónimo in Mexico City, as the portraits painted after those of her lifetime show her: a woman of about forty with a calm, clever oval face, in the habit of a Hieronymite nun, a white tunic with wide sleeves, a long black scapular and a black veil over a white wimple, with the large oval painted badge of her order worn high on her chest below the chin and a rosary at her waist. She stands at a tall shelf of leather-bound books that fills the wall behind her to the ceiling, one hand drawing a volume halfway out, turning to look at us over her shoulder as if interrupted in the middle of a thought. On a table beside her are a quill in an inkwell, a sheet of paper, a brass armillary sphere and a small table clock. Warm light from a high window. Seen from the waist up, her face in the upper right. New Spain in the late seventeenth century: no crown of flowers, no halo, no legible writing on the books or the paper. Keep the top left corner dark shelves.' },
    wuzetian: { cap: 'Luoyang, 624 to 705', ll: [112.44, 34.67], tx: 'When you play her, the strongest enemy Figure here misses its next turn.', fact: 'She rose from a junior place among the emperor\'s women to rule China, and in 690 took the throne in her own name. In all of China\'s history she is the only woman who reigned as emperor.', art: 'Wu Zetian in the year 690, aged sixty-six, on the day she takes the throne of China as emperor: a stately woman with a full, strong face, short broad painted brows and a small red flower mark between them, her black hair piled high and set with gold phoenix pins, in layered court robes of deep yellow and red, a wide-sleeved robe crossed at the chest over a long high-waisted skirt, with a long trailing sash. She stands at the top of a broad flight of palace steps with her hands folded inside her sleeves, looking down without expression. Below her on the steps three officials, one old and thin, one stout and one young, in black gauze caps with two short tails at the back and round-collared robes belted at the waist, are pressing their foreheads to the stone, and the young one, who has looked up too soon, is caught in her gaze. The courtyard beyond them is empty paving: no rows of kneeling figures. Red pillars rise behind her under a broad roof of dark gray tile with a gently curving ridge, in the Tang manner. Seen from the steps below, from the knees up, her face right of center, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Tang dynasty China: no Manchu headdress, no long nail guards, no dragon throne and no yellow glazed roofs of later dynasties. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only roof eaves and sky there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
    akbar: { cap: 'Fatehpur Sikri, 1542 to 1605', ll: [77.66, 27.09], tx: 'He has +2 Might while your Figures here outnumber enemy Figures.', fact: 'Emperor of most of India, he could not read, yet he kept a library of 24,000 books and had them read aloud to him every day. He called scholars of many faiths to his court to argue their beliefs in front of him.', art: 'Akbar, Mughal emperor of India, about 1580, listening to a night debate between the faiths in the hall he built for it at Fatehpur Sikri, drawn after the paintings of his own court: a sturdy, broad-chested man of thirty-eight with a moustache and no beard, his long hair gathered up under a small, tight, flat turban, in a plain white muslin coat tied at the side, seated on a low platform with one knee drawn up and his chin on his hand, listening hard. In a ring on the carpet below him sit five speakers, each in his own dress: a Muslim jurist in a large turban with a book open on a folding stand, a Hindu scholar bare to the waist with a sacred thread and a mark on his brow, a Jain monk in plain white with a shaven head, a Zoroastrian priest in white with a white cap, and a Jesuit father in a black gown and black cap, who has risen to his knees and is making his point with both hands. Oil lamps in niches of carved red sandstone. Seen from the edge of the ring, the emperor from the waist up, his face right of center, about a fifth of the way down the picture. Sixteenth-century Mughal India: no Taj Mahal, no later jewelled turbans with plumes. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner plain, with only carved red stone there: a plate sits in each top corner of the card.' },
  };
