  var P4 = {
    idrisi: { cap: 'Ceuta and Palermo, c. 1100 to 1165', ll: [13.36, 38.12], tx: 'When you play this Figure, you may look through your Domain stack and lay any one Domain at your Gate or your Outpost, in place of the Domain there. Then shuffle your stack.', fact: 'At the court of Roger II in Palermo he spent some fifteen years questioning travelers, and in 1154 he finished a map of the known world with a book describing every part of it.', sure: 3,
      art: 'Al-Idrisi about 1150, in his fifties, at the court of the Norman king of Sicily in Palermo. Nobody knows his face, so he is a thoughtful, weathered man with a grey-flecked beard, in the long robe and wound turban of a scholar from Muslim Spain and Morocco. He kneels on a carpet over a large round map drawn on parchment, with south at the top as his own maps have it, the sea in blue and the mountains as small colored humps, and marks a coast with a reed pen while he listens. He kneels on the right half of the picture. Two travelers kneel on the left half and answer his questions, each different: a sunburnt ship\'s pilot counting off harbors on his fingers, and a merchant in a fur-edged coat unrolling a strip of notes. Behind them a wall glitters with gold mosaic of the kind the king\'s chapel has. His head is three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. No globe, no compass rose, no north at the top. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain, with only plain gold mosaic ground there, no pictures, saints or faces in the mosaic anywhere: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    diogenes: { cap: 'Sinope and Corinth, c. 404 to 323 BC', ll: [22.93, 37.94], tx: 'While Diogenes is at a Domain, that Domain\'s power does nothing, for either player.', fact: 'He lived in Athens and Corinth with next to nothing and mocked wealth, rank and custom. The story goes that when Alexander offered him any favor, he asked the king to stand out of his sunlight.', sure: 1,
      art: 'Diogenes of Sinope in old age, about 330 BC, in the marketplace of Corinth: a lean, sunburnt, bald old man with a ragged white beard and bright, mocking eyes, barefoot, wearing only a coarse cloak folded double, with a stick and a small leather bag beside him. He sits on the ground in the morning sun, leaning back against the huge clay storage jar he sleeps in, which lies on its side and is as tall as his shoulder as he sits, and with one hand he waves someone out of his light: the long shadow of a tall standing man, who is not in the picture, falls across the ground in front of him and stops at his feet. A thin dog lies beside him. His head is three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Stone columns and market stalls behind, out of focus. A clay jar, not a wooden barrel. No lantern, no crown, no armor. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain, with only clear sky there: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    constantine: { cap: 'Naissus and Constantinople, c. 272 to 337', ll: [28.98, 41.01], tx: 'When you play this Figure, you may turn up the top Domain of your stack and lay it at the Crossroads, in place of the Domain there.', fact: 'In 330 he made the old Greek town of Byzantium, where Europe meets Asia, his new capital, and it was called Constantinople after him.', sure: 3,
      art: 'The emperor Constantine in the year 330, aged about fifty-eight, with the face of his colossal marble portrait in Rome: clean-shaven, a heavy square jaw, a strong hooked nose, large eyes looking up and far off, and hair combed forward in a fringe. He wears a band of pearls round his head, a long purple cloak pinned at the right shoulder with a jewelled brooch, and a gold-embroidered tunic. He stands on a terrace above the narrow blue strait at Byzantium with one hand held out flat, as if setting something down on the land below, where a new city is going up: a round forum with a tall column of dark red stone drums still in its scaffolding, and a line of new wall. Two surveyors work beside him, each different: an old one sighting along a cross-shaped surveying staff with hanging weights, a young one unrolling a plan. His head is three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. No halo, no cross in the sky, no domed church: none was built yet. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain, with only sky there: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    william: { cap: 'Normandy and England, c. 1028 to 1087', ll: [0.49, 50.91], tx: 'When you play this Figure at an opponent\'s Gate or Outpost, you may discard the Domain there. That player lays the top Domain of their stack in its place.', fact: 'After he won at Hastings in 1066 he gave the land of England to his own followers, and his great survey of 1086, the Domesday Book, shows almost none of it still in English hands.', sure: 3,
      art: 'William, duke of Normandy, at the battle of Hastings on 14 October 1066, at the moment the Bayeux Tapestry shows: his men think he is dead, and he pushes his helmet back to show them his face. A strongly built man of about thirty-eight with a heavy, clean-shaven face and his dark hair shaved high at the back of the head in the Norman way, in a knee-length shirt of mail and a cone-shaped iron helmet with a nose bar, which his left hand tips up and back. In his right hand he holds a wooden club, as the tapestry gives him, not a sword. He sits a stocky stallion, turning in the saddle toward us, and the horse is in true proportion: its back level with a standing man\'s chest, its head as long as his arm. Two riders beside him, each different: a bishop in mail with a club of his own, and a young knight with a long shield shaped like a kite, pointing at him and shouting. His head is three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. No plate armor, no crown, no castle. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain, with only gray sky there: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    wren: { cap: 'London, 1632 to 1723', ll: [-0.098, 51.514], tx: 'When you play this Figure, you may take a Domain from your Domain discard pile and lay it at your Gate or your Outpost, in place of the Domain there.', fact: 'After the Great Fire of London in 1666 he rebuilt St Paul\'s Cathedral and more than fifty of the city\'s churches.', sure: 3,
      art: 'Sir Christopher Wren about 1700, in his late sixties, on the scaffolding of St Paul\'s Cathedral as its dome goes up: a small, slight, alert man with a long narrow face and a full curled wig to his shoulders, in a plain dark coat with a white neck cloth, as his portrait by Kneller shows him. He stands on a plank platform with a pair of brass dividers in one hand and a large drawing held open against the wind in the other, looking up at the work, pleased. Behind him rises the ring of pale stone columns that will carry the dome, about eight times a man\'s height, wrapped in timber scaffolding and ropes. Two masons work beside him, each different: a broad one in a leather apron setting a block with a mallet, a boy steadying a pulley rope. Far below, the new brick city and the spires of his churches, and the river. His head is three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. No finished dome, no flames. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain, with only sky there: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    earthquake: { cap: 'Rhodes, 226 BC', ll: [28.23, 36.45], tx: 'Choose any player\'s Gate or Outpost, yours included. The Domain there is discarded, and the player whose side it is on lays the top Domain of their stack in its place.', fact: 'An earthquake in 226 BC threw down the Colossus of Rhodes, which had stood for little more than fifty years.', sure: 3,
      art: 'Rhodes in 226 BC, moments after the earthquake: the Colossus, the hollow bronze statue of the sun god seventy cubits high (nineteen times a man\'s height), has snapped at the knees and lies fallen on the harbor quay among the toppled white marble blocks of its base, as Pliny describes it lying. Only its lower legs still stand on the broken base in the distance. Its true size is the point of the picture, and it is easy to measure: a person\'s whole height is only about the length of one of the statue\'s fingers; its thumb is so thick that a man can barely wrap his arms round it; its hand is as long as two people lying end to end; its head, from chin to crown, is as tall as two and a half people standing one on another. Where the limbs broke off, the hollow insides gape like caves, and great rough stones and thick iron bars can be seen inside them. The sun god is a young man with thick wavy hair, and round his head is a halo of the sun: thirty or more very fine, thin bronze rays, short and close-set like the bristles of a brush, each about as long as a person is tall and no thicker than a person\'s arm, standing out from the hair all round the head, so that it reads as the blazing sun and not as a crown of spikes (no few long thick spikes, nothing like the Statue of Liberty\'s crown); he wears a belted tunic of bronze and a cloak pinned at the shoulder; no loincloth, no torch, no spiked band like a modern statue\'s crown. We stand low on the quay and look up at the fallen right hand, which lies palm up on top of a heap of toppled marble blocks, broken off at the wrist, the hollow wrist open toward us with stones and iron bars inside. It is a true human hand in proportion: four fingers curling up from the far end of the palm, the middle finger the longest, and the thumb growing from the side of the palm near the wrist, shorter than the fingers and pointing up and outward, with two joints and a nail. Beyond it the fallen body stretches away along the quay to the head with its halo of fine rays, lying face up. Dust is still rising off the fallen bronze and the broken marble, the sea behind is churned white and gray, ships lie wrecked and tilted in the harbor, and a low storm light breaks through dark cloud and lights the bronze. Three small people, each different: a sailor in a short tunic who stands on a block beside the upturned thumb and hugs it with both arms, his cheek against the bronze, his fingertips only just meeting behind it: the thumb is about as thick as his arm span is wide, and the thumb is a little taller than he is; a bald old man with a stick standing beside the end of the little finger, the little finger as long as he is tall; and a girl in a dusty dress climbing the blocks below the wrist. The great hand is the subject: the thumb and the sailor reaching round it are three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, with the fingertips clear of both top corners; the old man and the girl are lower down. No two people alike, and only these few people in the picture. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain, with only stormy sky there: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    vesuvius: { cap: 'Bay of Naples, AD 79', ll: [14.43, 40.82], tx: 'Lay Vesuvius on a Domain. While Vesuvius lies there, that Domain\'s power does nothing. If the Domain moves, Vesuvius moves with it, and if the Domain is discarded, so is Vesuvius.', fact: 'In AD 79 it buried the trading town of Pompeii in a day, and the town lay under the ash for nearly seventeen hundred years.', sure: 3,
      art: 'Vesuvius on the afternoon the eruption began in AD 79, seen from a street in Pompeii looking toward the mountain: a single green peak, and standing on it a cloud shaped as the one eyewitness described it, like an umbrella pine, a tall straight trunk of gray smoke that spreads into flat branches at the top, dark in some places and pale in others. Small light stones are starting to fall and bounce on the street, which is paved with dark blocks and crossed by a row of raised stepping stones. Three people hurry away toward us, each different, each with a cushion tied on the head with a strip of cloth, as the eyewitness says people did: a baker with a child on his hip, a woman carrying a small bronze lamp, and an old man with a stick. Shop counters with round holes for jars line the street. The top of the cloud is three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. No rivers of red lava, no two-peaked mountain. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain, with only pale sky there: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    caperoute: { cap: 'Lisbon to Calicut, 1498', ll: [75.78, 11.25], tx: 'Turn up the top Domain of your stack and lay it at the Crossroads, in place of the Domain there.', fact: 'Vasco da Gama reached India by sea around Africa in 1498, and within a few years a large share of the spice trade that had made Venice and Cairo rich went by the new route.', sure: 2,
      art: 'The end of the first voyage from Europe to India round Africa, off Calicut in May 1498: a Portuguese ship with a high stern, square sails with a red cross on the fore and main masts and a slanting sail at the back, about twenty-five paces long, coming to anchor under towering monsoon clouds, with a low green coast of coconut palms and blue hills behind it. A long, narrow local fishing boat has come alongside. Three people, each different: on the ship\'s rail a grey-bearded pilot in a turban, the man who showed the ships the way across from Africa, pointing at the shore; beside him a ship\'s boy, thin after ten months at sea, laughing; and in the fishing boat a fisherman of the coast in a waist cloth, standing easily with a paddle and looking up at them. The pilot\'s head is three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. No fort, no cannon fire, no flag being planted. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain, with only cloud there: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    breda: { cap: 'Breda, 1667', say: 'bray-DAH', ll: [4.78, 51.59], tx: 'Choose two Domains. They change places. Each one keeps its banner and anything lying on it, and the Figures stay where they are.', fact: 'By the Treaty of Breda in 1667 the Dutch kept the nutmeg island of Run, which they had taken from the English, and the English kept New Amsterdam, which they had taken from the Dutch and named New York.', sure: 3,
      art: 'The peace made at Breda in the Netherlands in July 1667, seen across a table in a room of the castle, at eye level: on the table, pushed toward each other from opposite sides, lie two things that are being exchanged. The table top is just above the middle of the picture, so the nutmeg and the map are in its upper half. One is a nutmeg split open, the brown seed in its bright red net, on a small silver dish. The other is a small hand-drawn map of an island at the mouth of a river, with a little walled town at its tip. Three people, each different: an English envoy in a long curled wig and a lace collar, one finger on the map; a Dutch envoy in plain black with a broad white collar, one finger on the dish; and between them a secretary dripping red wax onto a folded paper. Through the window, stepped gable roofs and a canal. The secretary\'s head is three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. No skyscrapers, no flags on the table. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain, with only dark panelling there: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    columbian: { cap: 'The Atlantic, from 1492', ll: [-5.99, 37.38], tx: 'Choose another player. The Domains at your Gate and theirs change places, and so do the Domains at your Outpost and theirs. Each one keeps its banner and anything lying on it, and the Figures stay where they are.', fact: 'After 1492 maize, potatoes and tomatoes crossed from the Americas to the rest of the world, and wheat, horses and cattle crossed the other way.', sure: 3,
      art: 'A quay on the river at Seville about 1530, with a ship being loaded and unloaded at the same time by one gangplank. Going down the plank to the shore: baskets of maize cobs, tomatoes, chilli peppers and brown cacao pods, and a live turkey under a sailor\'s arm. Going up the plank into the ship: sacks of wheat, bundles of sugar cane, and a small bay horse led by its halter, in true proportion to the man leading it, its back level with his chest. Three people, each different: the sailor with the turkey, grinning; the groom coaxing the horse; and a merchant in a dark gown and flat cap on the quay, holding a tomato up to the light, puzzled. A twelve-sided stone tower stands on the river bank behind. The merchant\'s head is three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. No soldiers, no gold, no map. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain, with only sky and rigging there: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    kudurru: { cap: 'Babylonia, c. 1200 BC', ll: [44.42, 32.54], tx: 'Opponents\' cards can\'t replace or move the Domains at your Gate and your Outpost, or switch off their powers.', fact: 'In Babylonia a grant of land was carved on a stone, with curses on anyone who moved the boundary or took the land.', sure: 3,
      art: 'A Babylonian boundary stone of about 1200 BC, standing in a niche of a mud-brick temple and lit by one small clay oil lamp no bigger than a hand, a tenth as wide as the stone, on the floor beside it: a smooth, dark, egg-shaped boulder of limestone about half a man\'s height. Round its top are carved the signs of the gods who guard the grant: a sun disc with wavy rays, a crescent moon, an eight-pointed star, a scorpion, and two empty thrones, each with a tall horned cap resting on its seat and no figure on it. A long snake is carved down one side from top to bottom. Below the signs the stone is covered with columns of small wedge-shaped writing. No people. Through the temple door behind it, small and bright, lies the land the stone speaks for: a barley field, a canal and a line of date palms. The top of the stone is three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. No Egyptian signs, no winged bulls. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain, with only shadowed brick there: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
  };
  P4.congress = { cap: 'Vienna, 1814 to 1815', ll: [16.37, 48.21], tx: 'Roll two dice, and the two Domains they name change places. The dice count across the map from your side: 1 is your Gate, 2 your Outpost, 3 the Crossroads, 4 the Outpost of the player on your left and 5 their Gate. A 6 is any Domain you choose. If both dice name the same Domain, it is discarded, and its owner lays the top Domain of their stack in its place.', fact: 'In 1814 and 1815 the powers that had beaten Napoleon met in Vienna and redrew the borders of Europe, handing lands from one crown to another.', sure: 3,
    art: 'The Congress of Vienna in the winter of 1814 to 1815, in a panelled room of the Austrian chancellery: a long table covered with a large map of Europe, on which the borders are being moved with pins, lengths of colored ribbon and small wax seals. The table top is just above the middle of the picture, so the map is in its upper half. Three statesmen lean over it, each different. The Austrian minister, a slim, elegant man of forty-one with fair curled hair, in a dark court coat with a star on the breast, holds a ribbon between two pins and smiles. The French envoy, a man of sixty with powdered hair, a pale, heavy-lidded face and a high neck cloth, leans on a cane with one hand and with the other slides a seal an inch across the map. The British minister, tall, handsome and stiff in a plain dark coat, folds his arms and frowns at the place the seal has left. Through a half-open door behind them, small and bright, a ballroom with one chandelier and two dancers. The Austrian minister\'s head is three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Nobody rolls dice: the chance on the card is the game\'s, and the Congress bargained. No Napoleon, no soldiers, no modern flags. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain, with only dark panelling there: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' };
  Object.keys(P4).forEach(function (k) { T[k] = P4[k]; if (P4[k].say) SAY[k] = P4[k].say; });
  Object.assign(SAY, { idrisi: 'al-ih-DREE-see', diogenes: 'dy-OJ-uh-neez', vesuvius: 'vuh-SOO-vee-us' });
  Object.assign(LINKS3, {
    "@constantinople>constantine": "In 330 Constantine made the old town of Byzantium his capital, and it took his name.",
    "@winchester>william": "William kept his treasury at Winchester, and the Domesday Book, his survey of England, was kept there too.",
    "@royalsociety>wren": "The Royal Society was founded in 1660 after a lecture that Wren gave, and he was later its president.",
    "@cape>caperoute": "Vasco da Gama's ships rounded the Cape of Good Hope in November 1497 on their way to India.",
    "@lisbon>caperoute": "Da Gama's ships sailed from Lisbon in July 1497 and came home to it in 1499 with pepper and cinnamon.",
    "@qhapaq>pachacuti": "The great Inca road grew with the conquests that Pachacuti began, and it tied his new empire to Cusco.",
    "@wien>congress": "The Congress met in Vienna from September 1814 to June 1815, and the city kept its guests busy with concerts and balls.",
    "@babylon>kudurru": "The kings of Babylon recorded their grants of land on such stones and put them under the protection of the gods."
  });
  // The Domains' own pronunciations (by Domain id)
  Object.assign(SAY, { malacca: 'muh-LAK-uh', kilwa: 'KIL-wah kee-see-WAH-nee', tlatelolco: 'tlah-teh-LOL-koh', djenne: 'jen-AY', laurion: 'LAW-ree-on', meroe: 'MEH-roh-ee', jingdezhen: 'jing-duh-JUN', benin: 'beh-NEEN', greenwich: 'GREN-ich', padua: 'PAD-yoo-uh', nalanda: 'nuh-LAHN-duh', thermopylae: 'thur-MOP-uh-lee', himeji: 'hee-MEH-jee', laferriere: 'sit-uh-DEL lah-fair-YAIR', orleans: 'or-lay-AHN', nanmadol: 'nahn mah-DOHL', sigiriya: 'see-gee-REE-yuh', rapanui: 'RAH-pah NOO-ee', heian: 'HAY-ahn kyoh', deirelbahari: 'dair el-bah-HAH-ree', versailles: 'vair-SIGH', giza: 'GEE-zuh', chichen: 'chee-CHEN eet-SAH', kumasi: 'koo-MAH-see', tordesillas: 'tor-deh-SEE-yas', runnymede: 'RUN-ee-meed', aachen: 'AH-ken', persepolis: 'per-SEP-uh-lis', onondaga: 'on-un-DAH-guh', lanse: 'LAN-see MED-ohz', dunhuang: 'dun-HWAHNG', appian: 'AP-ee-un', qhapaq: 'KAH-pak NYAN', basra: 'BAHS-ruh' });
  // ==P5==
  // ── The newest cards of the prime set (cards.js P5, 2026-11-13). Each fact and each link sentence has its source in
  // scripts/anoria/set-p5.json. No pictures yet: they are in NOPIC below and show as monograms until the art pass.
  var P5 = {
    biruni: { cap: 'Ghazni, 973 to c. 1050', ll: [68.42, 33.55], tx: 'Eureka! When you play a Relic, draw a card.', fact: 'He worked out the size of the Earth from one hilltop in the Punjab: first the hill\'s height, then how far the horizon dipped below level from its top. His answer was within about one percent of today\'s.', art: 'Al-Biruni about 1020 on the flat top of a rocky hill near the fort of Nandana in the Punjab, at dawn, measuring how far the horizon dips: a lean Persian scholar of about fifty with a short graying beard and a plain white turban, in a long dark coat, kneeling on one knee on the rock and holding up a brass astrolabe, a flat disk about as wide as his hand is long, hanging straight down by its ring from his raised left hand at arm\'s length in front of his face. The disk hangs edge-on to him and square to us, so that we see its whole round face: a thin flat brass sighting rule lies flat against that face, pivoted at the disk\'s center, and runs across the face from rim to rim, tipped a little downward toward the horizon on the right, ending at the rims in two small pierced vanes. The rule lies in the plane of the disk; nothing sticks out of the disk toward him or away from him. His open eye is just past the disk\'s left rim, in line with the rule, looking along it at the far horizon, his other eye closed; the fingertips of his right hand touch the rule\'s end at the rim to turn it. One young assistant beside him, different in age and dress, holds a wax tablet and a pen ready to write down the angle. Behind them the hill falls away steeply, and far below lies a wide, flat, hazy plain of dry fields and villages, plainly land and not sea, whose edge meets the sky in a long level line, a little below the level of his eye. Seen from beside the instrument, from low, so that the instrument, Al-Biruni\'s face and the falling hillside are all in the upper half of the picture, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only two people. No telescope, no glass lenses, no globe, no modern compass, no book with a printed cover. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    alrazi: { cap: 'Rayy and Baghdad, c. 865 to 925', ll: [51.43, 35.59], tx: 'Steadfast: +2 Might when defending.', fact: 'A physician who ran hospitals in Rayy and Baghdad, he wrote a book that taught doctors to tell smallpox from measles by the signs they could see. In his Doubts about Galen he set what he saw in his own patients against the great Greek doctor\'s teaching.', art: 'Al-Razi about 910 on the ward of the hospital at Baghdad, teaching at a bedside: a broad, gray-bearded Persian physician of about forty-five in a dark robe and a modest white turban, sitting on a stool beside a low bed and holding a sick boy\'s wrist to count his pulse, while he turns to speak to two students. The boy, about ten, sits propped on a cushion with a light rash of small red spots on his face and arms. The two students, a young man and an older one, each different, stand at the foot of the bed on the left of the picture, their heads at the same height as Al-Razi\'s and no higher than a third of the way down the picture; the young one holds a clear glass flask of the boy\'s water up to the window light to look at it. Plain whitewashed walls, a high arched window with a wooden lattice, a brass basin and jug on a shelf. The right side of the picture above and behind Al-Razi is plain whitewashed wall with nobody in it. Seen from across the bed, from the waist up, Al-Razi\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the boy and the flask also in the upper half of the picture. Only four people. No microscope, no stethoscope, no printed books, no modern bandages. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    ulughbeg: { cap: 'Samarkand, 1394 to 1449', ll: [67, 39.67], tx: 'Scholar: draw a card when this Figure is played.', fact: 'Ruler of Samarkand, he built an observatory around a giant curved track of stone cut into the hill, to sight the stars as they crossed the meridian. His catalog fixed the places of over a thousand stars, the best since ancient times.', art: 'Ulugh Beg about 1437 inside his observatory at Samarkand at night: a slim Timurid prince of about forty with a thin black mustache and short beard, in a fine patterned robe and a turban wound over a small cap, standing at the bottom of a wide stone trench that curves down into the hillside like a huge ramp, its two parallel rails of polished marble marked with degrees. He leans to look up along a sighting bar toward a bright star seen through a narrow slot in the roof high above. Beside him one astronomer, an older man with a long gray beard, holds a lamp low and reads out a number from a sheet. The two curved marble rails run from the bottom of the picture up and away to the top of it, each rail as wide as a man\'s hand and the two of them three times a man\'s width apart. Seen from beside the rails, looking up the slope, Ulugh Beg\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down and the star and slot in the upper half. Only two people. No telescope, no lenses, no domed planetarium, no globe. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    tycho: { cap: 'Hven, 1546 to 1601', ll: [12.69, 55.91], tx: 'When you play this Figure, you gain 1 Spark.', fact: 'The king of Denmark gave him a whole island, where he measured the planets night after night without a telescope, more exactly than anyone before him. He wore a nose of brass after losing his own in a duel.', art: 'Tycho Brahe about 1585 at his observatory on the island of Hven, at night, beside his great wall quadrant: a stout Danish nobleman of about forty with a broad reddish mustache curling out to the sides, a short pointed beard, a round face and a small brass false bridge on his nose, plain to see, in a black doublet with a white ruff and a gold chain, standing with one hand raised to give a signal. Beside him, indoors, a large brass quarter circle is fixed flat against a plain white wall, a little taller than a man (its radius about two metres). It is drawn exactly as a quarter of a circle: its center is at the top, level with Tycho\'s shoulder, just left of him, where a small square opening in the wall shows the night sky and a bright planet; from that center one straight brass edge runs level to the left and the other runs straight down, and the curved edge, engraved with fine degree marks, sweeps round between their two ends at the lower left. A young assistant in a plain coat crouches at the lower left by the curved edge, his eye at a small flat square brass plate with a narrow slit in it that slides along the arc, sighting up along the radius toward the center and the opening; there is no tube, no lens and no eyepiece, only the slit. A second assistant, older, sits at a small table at the right writing the number down by a candle. Seen from across the room, Tycho\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. No drawn lines of sight. A plain plastered room with a ceiling, not an open roof. Three people only. No telescope anywhere (it had not been invented), no glass lenses, no globe of the Earth. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    bassi: { cap: 'Bologna, 1711 to 1778', ll: [11.34, 44.49], tx: 'Scholar: draw a card when this Figure is played.', fact: 'In 1732 the university of Bologna made her a doctor and then a professor, the first woman in the world paid to teach at a university. She raised a family and ran a laboratory of electricity in her own home.', art: 'Laura Bassi about 1745 in the laboratory in her home at Bologna, giving a lesson: a woman of about thirty-five with dark hair pinned up under a small lace cap and a calm, firm face, in a plain dark silk dress with a white lace collar, standing at a long table and turning the crank of a glass globe electrical machine with one hand while she holds out a thin metal rod toward a dangling feather with the other; the feather leaps toward the rod. Two students watch closely from across the table, one a young man in a wig and a brown coat with his hand raised to ask a question, the other an older man bending close. On the table a glass jar half lined with foil, a brass prism stand, an open notebook. Tall windows, plain walls. Seen from across the table, from the waist up, her head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the glass globe and the rod in the upper half. Only three people. No light bulbs, no batteries, no modern wires. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    cook: { cap: 'Whitby and the Pacific, 1728 to 1779', ll: [-0.61, 54.49], tx: 'Voyager: can walk to any Place. Steadfast: +2 Might when defending.', fact: 'A farm laborer\'s son who learned the sea on coal ships, he charted New Zealand and Australia\'s east coast and sailed nearer the South Pole than anyone before. Sauerkraut and fresh food kept scurvy from his crews.', art: 'Captain James Cook in 1770 on the quarterdeck of the Endeavour, a small, broad, three-masted ship, taking the sun\'s height at noon: a tall, lean Englishman of forty-one with a long face, a strong straight nose and his own hair tied back, in a dark blue naval coat with white facings and no wig, holding a brass sextant up to his eye with both hands. Beside him at the rail one officer, a young man in a plain coat, writes the reading in a log book, and behind them a sailor at the big wooden wheel. Beyond the rail a green, wooded coast with a line of surf. The sails above are full and cream colored, the rigging tied at both ends. Seen from the deck, from a little below, Cook\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the sextant and the coast in the upper half of the picture. Three people only. No steam, no flags with stars, no modern binoculars, no telescope larger than a hand telescope. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    banneker: { cap: 'Maryland, 1731 to 1806', ll: [-76.79, 39.27], tx: 'Your Deeds cost 1 less.', fact: 'A free Black farmer who taught himself astronomy, he worked out a year of the sky for an almanac and helped survey the land for the new capital, Washington. He sent his almanac to Thomas Jefferson with a letter against slavery.', art: 'Benjamin Banneker in 1791 at night outside his log farmhouse in Maryland: a Black man of sixty with short white hair and a kind, steady face, in a plain brown coat and waistcoat, lying back on a blanket on the grass, his head and shoulders propped on a rolled coat with a small hand telescope, a short brass tube, pointed up at the stars, held to his eye with one hand, while a lantern at his side lights an open notebook ruled in columns. The lantern is the kind his time used: a punched-tin cylinder with a cone top and a ring handle, the candlelight coming out through its pattern of small holes; no glass globe, no wire guard, no kerosene lamp. The columns in the notebook are tiny marks that cannot be read, no numbers. Behind him a split-rail fence, an orchard tree and the small log house with one lit window. Above, a clear sky of stars and a crescent moon. Seen from beside him, from low, the telescope and his face in the upper half of the picture, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, with the sky above it. Only him. No modern clothes, no eyeglasses with wire frames, no large telescope on a tripod. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    mendeleev: { cap: 'Saint Petersburg, 1834 to 1907', ll: [30.31, 59.94], tx: 'When one of your Legacy Figures makes a Legacy Line, draw a card.', fact: 'He laid out the elements in a table by weight and by how they behave, and left gaps where nothing fit. He said what the missing elements would be like, and gallium, scandium and germanium were found to match.', art: 'Dmitri Mendeleev in 1869 at his desk in Saint Petersburg, at night: a Russian chemist of thirty-five with long, wild hair to his shoulders and a long, untrimmed beard, in a loose dark jacket, leaning over the desk and moving small white cards, each with an element\'s name and weight written on it, into rows and columns like a game of patience. He holds one card in the air, about to put it in an empty space in the pattern. Around him, glass flasks and a coal-oil lamp; his tea glass in a metal holder. Seen from across the desk, slightly from above, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the pattern of cards on the desk in the upper half. Only him. The cards are blank except for small marks that cannot be read: no letters or numbers that can be read anywhere. No modern periodic table poster, no electric light. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    ramanujan: { cap: 'Madras and Cambridge, 1887 to 1920', ll: [80.27, 13.08], tx: '+2 Might while outnumbered here.', fact: 'A clerk at the port of Madras with almost no training in mathematics, he mailed pages of his own formulas to a Cambridge professor in 1913. G. H. Hardy studied them with a colleague that evening and brought him to England.', art: 'Srinivasa Ramanujan in 1913 at Madras, in the evening, writing his letter: a young Tamil man of twenty-five, heavily built, with a full, round, smooth-shaven face, plump cheeks and a soft jaw, large dark heavy-lidded eyes and thick short black hair parted at the side, as in his photographs, his eyes lowered to the slate with a calm, absorbed look (not staring out at us), in a plain white shirt and a dhoti, sitting cross-legged on a mat on a veranda and writing on a small black slate balanced on his knee, with sheets of paper covered in formulas weighed down by a stone beside him. A brass oil lamp burns on the floor. Behind him a carved wooden pillar and the warm evening sky over flat roofs and palm trees. Seen from the front, a little from above, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the slate and papers in the upper half of the picture. Only him. The marks on the slate and paper are tiny curving squiggles that cannot be read as any letter, number or symbol. A respectful, recognizable likeness from his photographs. No suit and tie, no Cambridge gown. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    solon: { cap: 'Athens, c. 630 to 560 BC', ll: [23.73, 37.97], tx: 'Law: enemy Figures here can\'t clash.', fact: 'Chosen to mend Athens, where the poor were sold as slaves for their debts, he cancelled the debts and set them free. He wrote new laws on wooden boards for all to read, then left the city for ten years so nobody could make him change them.', art: 'Solon in 594 BC in the marketplace of Athens, with the new laws set up: a lean Athenian of about forty-five with a short dark beard going gray, in a plain wool cloak over a tunic, standing beside a wooden frame as tall as a man that holds a row of long four-sided wooden beams set upright on pivots, each face painted with lines of small marks that cannot be read, turning one of them on its pivot to show it. The frame stands at Solon\'s right, its top a third of the way down the picture, well clear of the top corners. One farmer in a rough tunic, freed from his debt, stands before him with his hand on his chest; a merchant in a better cloak frowns beside him. Behind, a low rocky hill with flat-roofed houses and one small, plain early temple of mud brick and timber with a low tiled roof and only two squat wooden columns at its porch; no colonnade around it, no marble, not the Parthenon. Seen from the front, from a little below, Solon\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the boards in the upper half. Three people only. No marble Parthenon, no building with columns all round it, no Roman togas, no scrolls in racks, no letters that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    ezana: { cap: 'Aksum, c. 320 to 360', ll: [38.72, 14.13], tx: 'When you play this Figure, you gain 1 Spark.', fact: 'King of Aksum, a trading power on the Red Sea, he was the first ruler of his land to take up Christianity, and his coins changed from the old sun and moon to a cross. He told of his wars on stones carved in Ge\'ez, Sabaean and Greek.', art: 'Ezana, king of Aksum, about 340, standing among the tall carved stone stelae of Aksum: an East African king of about thirty with dark brown skin, close-cut black hair and a short beard, in a white linen wrap with a gold-edged border over one shoulder and a high headcloth bound with a gold band, holding up a newly struck gold coin between finger and thumb to look at it in the sun. Beside him, a mint worker, an older man with a leather apron, holds a small leather bag of coins. Behind them two tall granite stelae carved with false windows and doors, the nearer three times a man\'s height, and a merchant\'s camel far off. Seen from below, the king\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the coin in the upper half against the sky. Only two people. The coin shows only a small cross and a head, no readable letters. No Roman armor, no Egyptian crowns, no modern Ethiopian dress. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    abbas: { cap: 'Isfahan, 1571 to 1629', ll: [51.68, 32.66], tx: 'When you play this Figure, you gain 2 Spark.', fact: 'He made Isfahan his capital and laid out at its heart a vast square for polo, markets and prayer, still one of the largest in the world. Armenian silk merchants, moved there by his order, made the city a center of the silk trade.', art: 'Shah Abbas the Great about 1619 on the high open balcony of the Ali Qapu palace above the great square of Isfahan: a Persian king of about fifty, a recognizable likeness from the portraits painted of him in his lifetime, with a long, drooping black mustache going gray and no beard, a strong nose and a sharp gaze, in a richly patterned silk coat and a tall turban with a plume, leaning on the balcony rail and pointing down toward the square. Beside him one Armenian silk merchant, an older bearded man in a dark coat and round cap, holds out a bolt of shining silk. Below and behind them stretches the long open square with a polo game far off (two riders small in the distance) and across the square the low cream-colored dome of a mosque patterned with blue vines, with two-storey arcades of shops along the square\'s sides. Seen from the balcony, Abbas\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only two people near us. No guns, no European dress, no domes with onion shapes. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    ieyasu: { cap: 'Edo, 1543 to 1616', ll: [139.75, 35.69], tx: 'Guard: enemies here must clash with this Figure first. Steadfast: +2 Might when defending.', fact: 'After a lifetime of war he waited, kept his alliances, and won Japan at the Battle of Sekigahara in 1600. The government his family founded at Edo, now Tokyo, kept the country at peace for over 250 years.', art: 'Tokugawa Ieyasu in 1600, the morning of Sekigahara, seated on a camp stool behind a white cloth screen marked with his family\'s three hollyhock leaves in a ring: a heavy-set Japanese warlord of fifty-seven with a round face, small eyes and a calm, patient expression, in dark lacquered armor laced with blue cord, his helmet resting at his feet, holding a war fan closed in one hand. Fog lies over the hills behind. One young messenger kneels before him with a folded letter, and one older general stands at his side watching the fog. Seen from the front, from a little below, Ieyasu\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only three people. No firearms in his hands, no castle, no crowds of soldiers, no Chinese dress. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    peter: { cap: 'Saint Petersburg, 1672 to 1725', ll: [30.33, 59.95], tx: 'Voyager: can walk to any Place. Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'Tsar of Russia, he traveled through Europe under a false name and worked with his own hands in a Dutch shipyard to learn how ships were built. Back home he built a navy and, on a marsh by the Baltic Sea, a new capital.', art: 'Peter the Great in 1697 in a Dutch shipyard, working in disguise: a very tall Russian of twenty-five, a recognizable likeness from Kneller\'s portrait of him painted in 1698, a head taller than anyone near him, with a round face, a small dark mustache, wide dark eyes and his own short curly dark hair, in a plain red workman\'s shirt with the sleeves rolled up and a leather apron, shaping a curved oak rib of a ship\'s hull that lies on tall trestles at the height of his waist, the adze lifted at the top of its swing in front of his chest. A shipwright\'s adze is not an axe: its curved iron blade is set crosswise to the handle, like a hoe, its edge at a right angle to the handle\'s line, and the adze head and his two hands are in the upper half of the picture, beside his face. A Dutch shipwright, a short older man with a pipe, looks up at him and points to where to cut next. Behind, the unfinished ribs of a ship on its slipway and the masts of finished ships beyond. Seen from a little below, a little way off, the whole of him from head to knees, Peter\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, with a fifth of the picture\'s height of plain sky above his head. Only two people. Peter is much taller than the shipwright, and his adze is the right size for a man: its handle about as long as his forearm and hand. No crown, no royal robes, no beard on Peter. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    moshoeshoe: { cap: 'Thaba Bosiu, c. 1786 to 1870', ll: [27.68, -29.36], tx: 'Guard: enemies here must clash with this Figure first. Steadfast: +2 Might when defending.', fact: 'In years of war and famine he gathered scattered peoples on the flat-topped mountain of Thaba Bosiu and made them one nation, the Basotho. Armies tried to storm the mountain again and again, and it never fell.', art: 'Moshoeshoe about 1840 at the edge of the flat top of Thaba Bosiu: a Basotho king of about fifty-five with a lean, thoughtful face, a short gray beard and close gray hair, wearing a kaross over one shoulder, a cloak of many silver-gray and tawny jackal skins sewn together, unspotted, and holding a long carved wooden staff, standing on the cliff edge and looking out calmly over the valley. Beside him one young warrior with a spear and a knobbed wooden club, carrying no shield, and one older counselor, each different. Far below, the plain green valley and the river; the mountain\'s sandstone cliffs drop straight down beneath them, with one narrow path cut up through a cleft. Seen from slightly below and to the side, Moshoeshoe\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, with a fifth of the picture\'s height of plain sky above his head, and the other two heads lower than his and away from the top corners, the cliff edge and the valley in the upper half. Three people only. No shields at all, no leopard skins, no European military uniforms, no rows of warriors. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    kamehameha: { cap: 'Hawaii, c. 1758 to 1819', ll: [-155.99, 19.64], tx: '+2 Might while your Figures here outnumber enemy Figures.', fact: 'He brought all the islands of Hawaii under one rule by 1810. His Law of the Splintered Paddle let the old, the young and every traveler lie by the road in safety, and it stands in Hawaii\'s constitution today.', art: 'Kamehameha I about 1810 at Kailua-Kona on the island of Hawaii: a very tall, powerfully built Hawaiian chief of about fifty, a recognizable likeness from Louis Choris\'s portrait of him drawn from life in 1816: a broad, heavy face, a strong brow, deep-set eyes, full lips and short cropped dark hair, wearing a long feather cloak of bright yellow with red borders over his shoulders and a plain loincloth, holding a long wooden spear upright. Beside him one chief, an older man in a short red feather cape, and one young paddler carrying a paddle. Behind, a stone platform with a thatched house, palms and the bright sea with one double-hulled canoe drawn up on black lava rock. Seen from a little below, Kamehameha\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only three people. The feather cloak hangs naturally to his calves; no crown, no European uniform, no flag. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    wells: { cap: 'Memphis and Chicago, 1862 to 1931', ll: [-90.05, 35.15], tx: 'When you play this Figure, the strongest enemy Figure here misses its next turn.', fact: 'A newspaper owner in Memphis, she went where Black men had been lynched and showed, case by case, that the excuses given for the killings were lies. A mob wrecked her press in 1892, and she carried on her work from the North.', art: 'Ida B. Wells in 1892 in her newspaper office in Memphis, at a composing case: a Black woman of thirty with a calm, determined face and her hair swept up, in a high-collared dark dress with a small brooch, standing at a wooden case of type and setting small metal letters into a hand-held composing stick, her eyes on a page of notes pinned beside the case. Behind her a hand press and a stack of printed sheets; one younger printer, a man in shirtsleeves, inks the press. Late light through a window. Seen from beside the case, her head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only two people. A respectful, recognizable likeness from her photographs. No letters on the sheets can be read; no crowd, no violence shown. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    piye: { cap: 'Napata, reigned c. 744 to 714 BC', ll: [31.83, 18.54], tx: 'Swift: can walk on the turn it\'s played.', fact: 'King of Kush, he marched north and made himself master of all Egypt. His victory stone tells how, in a captured city, he went to the stables and was more upset at the suffering of the horses than at the rebel prince who kept them.', art: 'Piye, king of Kush, about 728 BC in the royal stables of the captured city of Hermopolis: a Kushite king of about forty with dark skin, a close-fitting cap crown with two cobras rearing side by side at the brow (the double uraeus of the Kushite kings), a gold armlet and a pleated linen kilt, kneeling beside a thin, hungry bay horse, its ribs and hip bones showing, stroking its neck while he turns his head to frown hard at the defeated local prince. The prince, an Egyptian in a plain wig and kilt, bows low holding out the horse\'s lead. One stable boy holds a basket of fresh fodder. The horse is a horse of real size: its back is as high as a standing man\'s chest, and it has four legs. Mud-brick walls, wooden mangers, light from a doorway. Seen from beside the horse, Piye\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the horse\'s head beside his. Only three people and one horse. No camels, no stirrups, no saddles (they were not used yet), no chariots inside the stable. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    aethelflaed: { cap: 'Mercia, c. 870 to 918', ll: [-1.69, 52.63], tx: 'Guard: enemies here must clash with this Figure first.', fact: 'Alfred the Great\'s eldest daughter ruled the Midlands as Lady of the Mercians. She ringed her land with walled towns, and in 917 took Derby from the Vikings, the first of their Five Boroughs to fall.', art: 'Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians, in 917 on a new earth-and-timber rampart of one of her towns: an Anglo-Saxon woman of about forty-five with a strong, weathered face and fair hair braided under a white linen veil held by a plain circlet, in a long dark red dress with a cloak pinned by a round brooch at the shoulder, standing on the rampart walk with one hand on the sharpened top of the timber palisade and pointing out across the river with the other. Beside her one thegn in a mail shirt and plain helmet holding a spear, and one carpenter with a mallet, each different. Below, the fresh-dug ditch and the river. Seen from the walk, a little below her, her head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, with a fifth of the picture\'s height of plain sky above her head. The thegn stands just behind her shoulder, his head lower than hers and well away from the top left corner; the carpenter kneels at the palisade. Only three people. The palisade is one straight line of logs, all of a height, each as tall as a man. No stone castle, no plate armor, no horned helmets, no crown. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    tranhungdao: { cap: 'Đại Việt, c. 1228 to 1300', ll: [106.75, 20.9], tx: '+2 Might when attacking a Figure whose card shows more Might.', fact: 'Three times the Mongol armies came south, and three times Đại Việt turned them back. In 1288 he planted iron-tipped stakes in the Bạch Đằng River, drew the Yuan fleet in at high tide, and caught it there as the water fell.', art: 'Trần Hưng Đạo in 1288 on the bank of the Bạch Đằng River at dawn, watching the tide go out: a Vietnamese prince and general of about sixty with a long thin gray mustache and beard, in lacquered scale armor over a dark robe and a soft black cloth hat, holding a long-handled straight sword point down before him with both hands on the hilt. Beside him one young officer with a bow, and one older boatman with a long pole, each different. Below them the brown river runs out with the tide, and the tops of dark wooden stakes, each one leaning downstream, begin to show above the water in a broken line across the channel; beyond them three big Yuan junks with square ribbed sails come on toward the stakes. Seen from beside him on the bank, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the stakes and the junks in the upper half. Only three people near us. No Japanese armor, no conical straw hats on the officers, no rows of soldiers. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    babur: { cap: 'Kabul and Agra, 1483 to 1530', ll: [76.97, 29.39], tx: '+2 Might while outnumbered here.', fact: 'King of a small valley at eleven, he lost his throne and won it back more than once before he took Kabul. At Panipat in 1526 his small army beat a far larger one and began the Mughal Empire, and his own memoir tells the whole story.', art: 'Babur in 1526 in his camp tent the evening before Panipat, writing his memoir: a Central Asian prince of forty-three with a short, trimmed black beard and mustache, a lively, sharp face, in a quilted riding coat and a turban wound around a pointed cap, sitting cross-legged on a rug and writing with a reed pen in a bound book on his knee by the light of a small open clay oil lamp with one flame (no glass chimney, no lantern), pausing to look up through the open tent flap. Outside, small in the distance, a line of plain wooden carts chained wheel to wheel, with dark bronze gun barrels lying on low wooden beds between them (no spoked gun carriages). One young page, a boy of about twelve, holds the inkpot. Seen from inside the tent, Babur\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the tent flap and the camp beyond in the upper half. Two people only. No elephants in the camp, no gold crown, no later Mughal court jewels. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    cuauhtemoc: { cap: 'Tenochtitlan, c. 1497 to 1525', ll: [-99.13, 19.43], tx: 'Steadfast: +2 Might when defending.', fact: 'The last ruler of the Aztec city of Tenochtitlan, he held it for nearly three months against Spanish soldiers and their many native allies, through smallpox and hunger. He was taken at last in a canoe on the lake, on 13 August 1521.', art: 'Cuauhtémoc in 1521 on a causeway of Tenochtitlan during the siege: a young Mexica ruler of about twenty-four with a lean, determined face, black hair cut straight at the brow, wearing a quilted cotton armor tunic, a turquoise-green feather back ornament and a gold lip plug, holding a wooden club edged with dark obsidian blades and a round feather shield. Beside him one older warrior in an eagle helmet, and one young canoe-man with a paddle, each different. Behind, the water of the lake, a canoe, and the city\'s white temple pyramid with two shrines on its top. Seen from a little below, Cuauhtémoc\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only three people. No horses, no steel weapons in Mexica hands, no Plains headdresses, no rows of warriors. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    tupacamaru: { cap: 'Tinta, 1738 to 1781', ll: [-71.4, -14.15], tx: '+1 Might for each of your other Figures here.', fact: 'A mule-train owner descended from the last Inca ruler, he led the greatest uprising against Spanish rule in the Andes in 1780, to end forced labor in the mines. He and his wife, Micaela Bastidas, were executed in Cusco the next year.', art: 'Túpac Amaru II in 1780 in the high Andes above Tinta: a mestizo leader of forty-two with long black hair, a narrow face and dark eyes, in a dark velvet coat with lace cuffs, a three-cornered hat and an Inca sun emblem of gold on a chain at his chest, standing on a stone wall and raising one arm to call. Beside him Micaela Bastidas, his wife, about thirty-five, in a wide skirt and a woven shawl, holding a letter; and one Quechua farmer with a sling, each different. Behind, terraced green slopes and snow peaks. Seen from below, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only three people. No rows of fighters, no guns raised, no Plains headdresses. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    garibaldi: { cap: 'Marsala, 1807 to 1882', ll: [12.44, 37.8], tx: 'Swift: can walk on the turn it\'s played. Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'In 1860 he sailed to Sicily with about a thousand volunteers in red shirts and, against an army many times larger, won Sicily and Naples for a united Italy. Then he handed what he had won to the king and went home to his farm.', art: 'Giuseppe Garibaldi in 1860 on a Sicilian hillside, leading his volunteers forward: an Italian of fifty-three, a recognizable likeness from his photographs of 1860 with reddish-gray hair to his collar and a full reddish-gray beard, in a loose red woolen shirt, a gray South American poncho thrown over one shoulder and a round black felt hat, pointing a cavalry saber forward at shoulder height toward the hill ahead, the blade level and well below the top of the picture, away from both top corners. Beside him two volunteers, each different: a thin young student in a red shirt carrying a long musket and a sturdy older farmer in a red shirt with a cap. Behind, terraced hills with prickly pear and an old stone farmhouse. Seen from below, Garibaldi\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only three people. No later uniforms, no tricolor flags waved in rows, no rows of soldiers. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    qiujin: { cap: 'Shaoxing, 1875 to 1907', ll: [120.58, 30], tx: 'Swift: can walk on the turn it\'s played.', fact: 'A poet who left an arranged marriage to study in Japan, she trained with the sword, wore men\'s clothes and wrote for women\'s rights. She ran a school in Shaoxing that trained revolutionaries, and was executed in 1907, four years before the empire fell.', art: 'Qiu Jin in 1907 in the yard of her school at Shaoxing, at sword practice: a Chinese woman of thirty-one with an oval face and a firm mouth, her hair pulled back in a bun, in a dark Western-style man\'s suit jacket over a high-collared shirt, holding a straight, double-edged Chinese jian in her right hand only, in a guard at shoulder height: a short one-hand grip, a small crossguard bent toward the blade and a red tassel at the pommel, no round guard, no long two-hand grip; her left hand is raised in the sword-finger sign, two fingers pointing. One young student, a boy of about eighteen in a plain jacket, faces her with a wooden practice sword, and one older teacher watches from the veranda. Whitewashed walls, a tiled roof, a willow. Seen from the side, a little below, her head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only three people. A respectful, recognizable likeness from her photographs. No Japanese sword or kimono, no modern guns, no flags. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    zapata: { cap: 'Morelos, 1879 to 1919', ll: [-98.98, 18.77], tx: '+3 Might at home instead of +1.', fact: 'A horse trainer from a farming village in Morelos, he led its farmers in the Mexican Revolution to win back the land the great sugar estates had taken. His Plan of Ayala of 1911 asked above all for land for those who work it.', art: 'Emiliano Zapata in 1911 in the fields of his village in Morelos: a Mexican of thirty-two with a lean face, dark eyes and a great sweeping black mustache, in a short black charro jacket, white shirt, a wide sombrero, and a cartridge belt across his chest, standing beside his horse and holding a paper deed in one hand. One old farmer in white cotton clothes and a straw hat stands with him pointing to the fields. The horse is real size: its back is as high as Zapata\'s shoulder, and it has four legs. Behind, green cane fields and a distant smokestack of a sugar mill against the volcano. Seen from a little below, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only two people and one horse. A respectful, recognizable likeness from his photographs. No rows of fighters, no letters that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    augustine: { cap: 'Hippo, 354 to 430', ll: [7.77, 36.9], tx: 'If you hold this Figure\'s Place when a round ends, you draw a card.', fact: 'Born in North Africa, he taught speech-making in Milan and became bishop of Hippo. His Confessions, the story of his own restless youth and slow change of heart, is one of the first autobiographies and is still read today.', art: 'Augustine about 397 in a plain room of his house at Hippo on the North African coast, writing the Confessions: a Roman African man of forty-three with olive-brown skin, close-cropped dark hair going gray and a short beard, in a plain dark wool tunic and cloak, sitting at a small table by a window and dictating, one hand raised as he searches for a word. A young secretary sits opposite, writing on a wax tablet with a stylus. Through the window, the sea and a palm. Seen from across the table, Augustine\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only two people. No bishop\'s miter, no halo, no bound printed books, no gold. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    hafez: { cap: 'Shiraz, c. 1325 to 1390', ll: [52.55, 29.62], tx: 'When you play this Figure, you gain 1 Spark, or 2 if an opponent holds this Place.', fact: 'His short love poems, ghazals full of wine, roses and nightingales, made him Persia\'s best-loved poet. Families in Iran still open his book at random to see what a poem says about a question on their minds.', art: 'Hafez about 1370 in a walled garden at Shiraz in the evening: a Persian poet of about forty-five with a lean, humorous face, a short dark beard and a modest white turban, in a long plain robe, sitting on a carpet beneath a cypress tree with a small book of his own poems open on his knee, reciting to one listener, a young man, who leans forward smiling. A rosebush in flower beside them, a nightingale on a branch, a small cup and a jug on the carpet. Seen from the front, a little below, Hafez\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only two people. No portrait miniature style, no later Qajar dress, no letters that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    zeami: { cap: 'Kyoto, c. 1363 to 1443', ll: [135.77, 35.01], tx: 'If you hold this Figure\'s Place when a round ends, you score 1 Renown.', fact: 'At twelve he danced before the shogun, who took him into his court. As actor, writer and teacher he made Noh the theater it still is, and wrote its secrets down for his heirs: that an actor\'s \'flower\' is the wonder he brings.', art: 'Zeami about 1400 on the open stage of a shrine, a square floor of plain cypress boards under its own roof, dancing: a Japanese actor of about thirty-seven, slim and still, in a small carved wooden woman\'s mask with a pale face, a little smaller than his own face so his own chin shows below its edge, a wig of long straight black hair parted in the middle and falling down his back, white split-toed tabi socks on his feet, and a heavy embroidered silk robe of gold and red, one foot sliding forward and a folding fan held out level. Behind the stage grows one old pine tree with wide, flat branches. At the back of the stage one drummer kneels in plain dark robes, playing the kotsuzumi: a small hourglass-shaped drum, its two lacquered heads laced together with orange cords, held up on his right shoulder by his left hand and struck with the fingers of his right hand. No barrel drum, no drum on the floor. Seen from the front of the stage, from a little below, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only two people. The stage is one square floor with four pillars, drawn in one perspective. No audience in rows, no kabuki makeup, no later colored lights. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    garcilaso: { cap: 'Cusco and Córdoba, 1539 to 1616', ll: [-71.97, -13.53], tx: 'Scholar: draw a card when this Figure is played.', fact: 'Son of a Spanish captain and an Inca noblewoman, he grew up in Cusco hearing his mother\'s family tell of their empire. In Spain he wrote it all down, and his Royal Commentaries of the Incas of 1609 were read all over Europe.', art: 'Inca Garcilaso de la Vega about 1605 in his study at Córdoba in Spain: a man of sixty-five with a long, thoughtful face, brown skin and a short white beard, in a black Spanish doublet with a plain white collar, writing at a table with a quill. On the table beside him lies a quipu, a bundle of knotted colored cords, which he touches with his other hand as he remembers. On the wall, a small woven Andean cloth. Through a window, a whitewashed Spanish street. Seen from across the table, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only him. No Inca gold crown on him, no feathers, no letters that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    rembrandt: { cap: 'Amsterdam, 1606 to 1669', ll: [4.9, 52.37], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'The greatest painter of the Dutch golden age showed people as they really were, lit out of the dark, and painted, etched and drew himself some eighty times as he aged. He taught dozens of pupils, and still went bankrupt in 1656.', art: 'Rembrandt about 1640 in his studio in Amsterdam: a Dutch painter of thirty-four with a broad face, a bulbous nose, a small mustache and curly brown hair under a dark beret, in a working smock, standing at his easel with a palette and brushes, turning to show a young pupil how to lay a stroke of light on a face in the painting. The pupil, a boy of about sixteen with an apron, watches. A single high window throws a warm light across them; the rest of the room falls into dark. Seen from beside the easel, Rembrandt\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only two people. The painting on the easel shows a face in light and shadow, seen at an angle; no letters, no copy of The Night Watch. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    vivaldi: { cap: 'Venice, 1678 to 1741', ll: [12.34, 45.44], tx: 'Swift: can walk on the turn it\'s played.', fact: 'A red-haired priest who taught music at a Venice home for orphan girls, he wrote hundreds of concertos for them to play. A visitor wrote that he could compose a concerto faster than a copyist could copy it out.', art: 'Antonio Vivaldi about 1725 in the music room of the Pietà in Venice, rehearsing: a thin Venetian priest of forty-seven with red hair to his shoulders, a pale face with a long hooked nose and a pointed chin, as Ghezzi\'s caricature of 1723 drew him, and a black priest\'s cassock, standing with his violin under his chin and bow raised, leading. Facing him, three young women of the Pietà orchestra, each different in age and face, in plain dark dresses with white collars, play a violin, a cello and a lute; music stands before them. Tall window, canal light on the ceiling. Seen from beside the players, Vivaldi\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Four people only. The violin rests on his left shoulder under his chin, his left hand on its neck and the bow in his right hand; each violin has four strings, the cello stands on the floor between its player\'s knees, and the lute has a pegbox bent back. No powdered wig, no audience, no letters on the music that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    liliuokalani: { cap: 'Honolulu, 1838 to 1917', ll: [-157.86, 21.31], tx: 'Steadfast: +2 Might when defending.', fact: 'The last queen of Hawaii was overthrown in 1893 by American businessmen backed by United States marines, and later held prisoner in her own palace. She wrote more than 160 songs, among them Aloha ʻOe.', art: 'Queen Liliʻuokalani about 1893 in the music room of ʻIolani Palace in Honolulu: a Hawaiian woman of fifty-five with a dignified, strong face and her dark hair dressed high, in a black silk gown with a high collar and a small brooch, seated at a square piano with one hand on the keys and the other holding a pencil over a sheet of music, looking up toward a tall window. Through the window, palm trees and a gray afternoon. One lady-in-waiting, younger, in a plain dark day dress with a high collar, stands at the right side of the picture by the window, a few steps behind the queen, holding a closed fan, her head a little lower than the queen\'s and a third of the way down the picture. Seen from beside the piano, the queen\'s head just left of the middle of the picture and a quarter of the way down; the top left of the picture is plain wall. Only two people. A respectful, recognizable likeness from her photographs. No crown, no marines shown, no letters on the music that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    asmau: { cap: 'Sokoto, 1793 to 1864', ll: [5.24, 13.06], tx: 'Scholar: draw a card when this Figure is played.', fact: 'A princess of the Sokoto Caliphate who wrote poems in Arabic, Fulfulde and Hausa, she trained women teachers called jajis. They walked from village to village teaching her verses by heart, and women in Nigeria still learn that way.', art: 'Nana Asma\'u about 1830 in the courtyard of her house at Sokoto: a Fulani scholar of about thirty-seven with a long, calm face and fine features, her head wrapped in a pale indigo cloth, in a long plain robe, sitting on a mat and reciting from a handwritten book on a low wooden stand, one hand marking the rhythm. Before her, in the front of the picture and lower down, sit two jajis, women teachers, each different, their heads no higher than Nana Asma\'u\'s chest: at the lower left an older woman seen from behind and to the side, in a tall hat bound with a red turban cloth, and at the lower right a young woman holding a wooden writing board. The top half of the picture is Nana Asma\'u, the mud wall and the sky. Mud walls with rounded tops, a calabash and a clay water pot. Seen from beside the mat, her head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only three people. No rows of pupils, no letters that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    tagore: { cap: 'Santiniketan, 1861 to 1941', ll: [87.68, 23.68], tx: 'Scholar: draw a card when this Figure is played. Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'Poet, painter and composer of Bengal, he turned his own songs into English, and in 1913 Gitanjali made him the first writer from outside Europe to win the Nobel Prize in Literature. His songs are the national anthems of India and Bangladesh.', art: 'Rabindranath Tagore about 1913 teaching under a tree at his open-air school at Santiniketan: a Bengali poet of fifty-two with long wavy gray hair and a long gray beard, in a loose dark robe, sitting on a low stone platform around the trunk of a big tree and reading from a notebook, while three pupils, each different, two boys and a girl of about ten to fourteen in plain white cotton, sit on the ground before him, one with a slate. Dappled light, red earth. Seen from a little below, Tagore\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only four people. A respectful, recognizable likeness from his photographs. No rows of pupils, no school building with desks, no letters that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    nefertiti: { cap: 'Amarna, c. 1370 to c. 1330 BC', ll: [30.9, 27.65], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'She was queen beside the pharaoh Akhenaten when he moved Egypt\'s capital to a new city and its worship to the sun\'s disk. Her painted bust, found in a sculptor\'s workshop there in 1912, is one of the best-known faces of the ancient world.', art: 'Nefertiti about 1345 BC on a palace balcony at Amarna, in the morning sun: an Egyptian queen of about thirty, slender, with a long neck and fine features, wearing her own tall flat-topped blue crown with a gold band and a cobra at the brow, a broad bead collar of many colors and a pleated white linen dress, raising one hand palm up to the rays of the sun disk, each ray ending in a small hand, as Amarna art shows it. The sun disk is small, no wider than a sixth of the picture, in the middle of the sky above and in front of her: its middle exactly halfway across the picture and a fifth of the way down, with plain sky all round it and nothing at all in the top left part of the picture; its straight rays slant down and to the left to her open hand, which is raised in front of her face at the height of her crown. Below the balcony one young courtier bows. Behind, white walls painted with plants, and the cliffs of the desert beyond. Seen from below, her head and crown two thirds of the way across the picture and a third of the way down, with plain sky above the crown. Her crown is blue, the deep blue of the Berlin bust. Only two people. Her face as the Berlin bust shows it. Only date palms grow there, no cypress trees. No pyramids, no Nile crocodile gods, no sphinx. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    sixsky: { cap: 'Naranjo, died 741', ll: [-89.25, 17.13], tx: 'Raider: +2 Might when attacking on an opponent\'s side.', fact: 'Sent from Dos Pilas in 682 to restore the Maya city of Naranjo, she ruled it for her young son and led its wars. On her stone portraits she stands on the back of a captive, dressed as the maize god.', art: 'Lady Six Sky about 699 on the steps of a temple-pyramid at Naranjo, at a victory ceremony: a Maya noblewoman of about thirty-five with a sloping profile, strong features and long black hair, wearing a tall headdress of green quetzal feathers with a jade mask at its front, a net skirt of jade beads like the maize god\'s, and heavy jade ear spools, holding a small round shield and a ceremonial staff. One kneeling captive before her, his arms bound, and one young attendant holding a feather fan, each different. Behind, the white stucco of the temple and the green forest. Seen from below the steps, her head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only three people. No Aztec dress, no Plains headdress, no gold crown, no blood shown. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    zubaidah: { cap: 'The pilgrim road to Mecca, died 831', ll: [41.95, 27.12], tx: 'Can\'t be moved. +1 Might for each round this Figure stays, up to +2.', fact: 'Wife of the caliph Harun al-Rashid, she paid for wells, cisterns and resting places along the long pilgrim road from Kufa across the desert to Mecca, so that no pilgrim would die of thirst. The road is still called by her name.', art: 'Zubaidah about 800 at a newly built stone cistern on the pilgrim road across the Arabian desert: an Abbasid queen of about thirty-five with a fine, proud face, in a long silk robe of deep green and a fine veil over her hair held by a jeweled band, standing on the cistern\'s rim and looking down at the water. Close beside her, at the height of her waist, the end of a stone channel pours a bright stream of water over the rim into the cistern. One engineer, an older man in a plain robe and turban holding a measuring rod, stands with her, and one young pilgrim kneels at the channel\'s mouth to drink with cupped hands. The cistern is a round stone tank as wide as six men lying end to end, with steps down into it. Behind, sand, a few camels far off and the line of the road. Seen from close by, from a little below the rim: Zubaidah seen from the knees up, her head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down with plain sky above it, and the pouring channel and the water in the upper half of the picture, right beside her. Only three people. No palace, no Ottoman dress, no letters that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    sinan: { cap: 'Istanbul, c. 1490 to 1588', ll: [28.96, 41.02], tx: 'Can\'t be moved. +1 Might for each round this Figure stays, up to +2.', fact: 'A soldier who built bridges for the army, he became the Ottoman sultans\' chief architect for fifty years and designed hundreds of buildings. He called the Selimiye Mosque at Edirne, its great dome on eight pillars, his masterwork.', art: 'Mimar Sinan about 1557 on a timber scaffold on the outside of the half-built dome of the Süleymaniye Mosque in Istanbul: an Ottoman architect of about sixty-five with a long white beard and a tall white turban, in a dark fur-lined robe, holding a knotted measuring cord stretched taut between his two hands, both ends in his hands, while a stone mason, a younger man in a short jacket, sets a cut stone on the dome\'s unfinished upper edge beside him. The dome is seen from outside: one great rounded shell of cut stone laid in even rings, rising from a ring of small arched windows at its foot and curving up and inward like the top of an egg, and stopping short of its crown at an unfinished, level edge where the two men work, with the open sky above that edge. Beyond, far below, the roofs of the city and the water of the Golden Horn. Seen from the scaffold, Sinan\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the curve of the dome in the upper half. Only two people. No modern cranes, no battlements, no finished minarets, no rows of workers. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    leoafricanus: { cap: 'Fez and Rome, c. 1494 to c. 1554', ll: [-4.99, 34.03], tx: 'Voyager: can walk to any Place. Scholar: draw a card when this Figure is played.', fact: 'Born al-Hasan al-Wazzan in Granada and raised in Fez, he went as a diplomat as far as Timbuktu. Corsairs seized him in 1518 and gave him to the Pope, and in Rome he wrote the book that taught Europe about Africa for two centuries.', art: 'Leo Africanus about 1510 arriving at Timbuktu with a salt caravan: a young Moroccan diplomat of about sixteen with a narrow, alert face and a first beard, in a white turban and a long striped cloak over a robe, standing beside his camel and writing in a small notebook as he looks up at the city. Beside him one Tuareg guide with an indigo veil over his face, holding the camel\'s lead, and one Timbuktu scholar in white robes coming to greet them, each different. Behind, the great mud-brick mosque of Djinguereber with wooden beams standing out of its walls. The camel is real size: its hump is higher than the men\'s heads, and it has four legs. Seen from beside the camel, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only three people and one camel. No European dress, no letters that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    hasekura: { cap: 'Sendai, 1571 to 1622', ll: [140.87, 38.27], tx: 'Voyager: can walk to any Place. When you play this Figure, you gain 1 Spark.', fact: 'A samurai sent by his lord to seek trade with Spain, he crossed the Pacific, Mexico and the Atlantic, and met the Pope in Rome in 1615. When he came home seven years after he set out, Japan had turned against foreigners and their faith.', art: 'Hasekura Tsunenaga in 1615 arriving in Rome: a samurai of forty-four with a long, serious face, a thin mustache and his hair in a topknot, wearing a patterned silk kimono and wide trousers with two swords at his belt, standing on the steps of a Roman palace and looking up in wonder at a baroque church front. Beside him one Spanish friar in a brown habit, his guide and interpreter, and one young Japanese attendant carrying a lacquered letter box, each different. Seen from the steps below, Hasekura\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down. Only three people. No Chinese dress, no ninja clothes, no armor, no crowds. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    tupaia: { cap: 'Raiatea, c. 1725 to 1770', ll: [-151.44, -16.83], tx: 'Voyager: can walk to any Place.', fact: 'A priest and navigator from Raiatea, he joined Cook\'s Endeavour in 1769 and guided it through the islands he knew. For its officers he drew a chart of the Pacific around Tahiti that named seventy-four islands.', art: 'Tupaia in 1769 in the great cabin of the Endeavour, drawing his chart: a Tahitian priest and navigator of about forty-five with a strong, broad face, dark wavy hair tied up in a knot and tattoo marks on his hands, in a wrap of white bark cloth, leaning over a wooden table and drawing islands in ink on a large sheet, pointing to one of them while he names it. Beside him a young English officer in a blue coat writes the name down, and through the stern windows behind them lies the bright sea. Seen from across the table, Tupaia\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the chart in the upper half, seen at an angle so its small circles and lines can\'t be read as letters. Only two people. No feathered headdress, no grass skirt, no Maori facial tattoo. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    seondeok: { cap: 'Gyeongju, reigned 632 to 647', ll: [129.22, 35.83], tx: 'Scholar: draw a card when this Figure is played.', fact: 'The first woman to rule the Korean kingdom of Silla, she sent scholars to study in Tang China. Cheomseongdae, the stone tower raised in her reign to watch the sky, still stands, the oldest of its kind in East Asia.', art: 'Queen Seondeok about 640 at night before the stone tower of Cheomseongdae at Gyeongju: a Korean queen of about forty-five with a calm, intelligent face, in a layered silk robe of red and gold with wide sleeves and a gold crown of the Silla kind: a gold band with upright gold shapes like branching trees and antlers, hung with small comma-shaped jade pieces and tiny gold spangles, no crosses. She stands in front of the tower and nearer to us, seen from the waist up, her head and crown three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, with plain night sky above her crown. Behind her and to the left, farther off, the tower: a single bottle-shaped tower of fitted granite blocks, round in plan, its sides curving inward as they rise like the neck of a bottle, topped by a square frame of two layers of crossing stone beams, with one square window halfway up; because it is farther away, its top is level with her crown. One astronomer, an older man in a plain robe, small with the distance, leans out of the window and points up at a bright star high in the sky between the tower and the queen. Only two people. No Chinese emperor\'s crown, no Japanese dress, no telescope. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    greekfire: { cap: 'Constantinople, 672', ll: [28.98, 41.01], tx: 'Your Figures have +1 Might when defending at your Gate or your Outpost.', fact: 'About 672 a refugee named Kallinikos brought the Byzantines a fire that burned even on water, pumped through a bronze tube at enemy ships. Its recipe was kept so secret that it is lost to this day.', art: 'Greek fire in use in the 670s at sea off Constantinople: the bow of a Byzantine war galley, a dromon, seen from the side, its long oars dipping, and in its bow a long, slim bronze-covered tube, the siphon, resting on a swivel on the bow rail. One sailor in a leather cap stands beside it and aims it with both hands on two handles fixed to its side, his face well back from it. It does not look like a cannon: no wheels, no gun carriage, no flared muzzle, no thick breech. A leather hose runs from its back end down to a bronze tank on the deck. From its mouth a long jet of yellow-orange fire shoots out flat across the water toward the dark hull of an enemy ship. A second sailor behind him pushes a long wooden lever, the handle of the force pump on the bronze tank, up and down. The flames run across the surface of the sea between the two ships. Seen from low on the water, the tube and the jet of fire in the upper half of the picture, the jet\'s mouth three fifths of the way across and a quarter of the way down. Two people only. Open sea behind, no city, no domes, no minarets. The galley is one ship with a single row of oars on our side, all the same length; no cannons, no gunpowder smoke, no sails of later ships. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    rockets: { cap: 'Mysore, 1780', ll: [76.69, 12.42], tx: 'Once a turn, one of your Figures can start a clash with an enemy Figure at the next Domain, without moving. If your Figure loses, nothing happens to it.', fact: 'The armies of Hyder Ali and Tipu Sultan of Mysore fired rockets with iron cases that could fly a kilometer or more. Captured ones were taken to Britain, where William Congreve studied them and built his own.', art: 'A Mysore rocket being launched about 1780: a rocketeer of the Mysore army, a lean man of about thirty in a short tiger-striped tunic and a turban, kneeling on dry ground and lighting the fuse of a rocket that rests on a low wooden stand, its body a small black iron tube only as long as his forearm from elbow to wrist and only as thick as his wrist, with a short pointed iron nose, lashed with cord to the front end of a long straight bamboo pole about four times as long as the tube. The tube is at the very front: nothing sticks out past its nose, and the pole trails back behind it to the ground like the stick of a firework rocket. The whole rocket is pointed up into the sky at an angle, and he holds a smoldering slow match to the short fuse coming out of the back end of the iron tube. A second rocket of the same kind, small and far off, already rises in an arc with its bamboo pole trailing behind it and a trail of white smoke. One older officer in a turban stands behind with a raised hand to give the signal. Behind, the dusty plain and a fort\'s walls far off. Seen from low beside the stand, the rocket and the smoke trail in the upper half, the rocket\'s tip three fifths of the way across and a quarter of the way down. Two people only. No modern rockets, no fins, no European uniforms. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    chronometer: { cap: 'London, 1761', ll: [-0.13, 51.51], tx: 'Once a turn, jump one of your Figures to any Place. It can\'t act again this turn.', fact: 'John Harrison, a carpenter, spent most of his life building clocks that keep time at sea. On a voyage to Jamaica in 1761 his fourth, a large pocket watch, lost only about five seconds, enough to find a ship\'s longitude.', art: 'Harrison\'s fourth sea watch, H4, in 1761 on its trial voyage to Jamaica aboard HMS Deptford, in the hand of William Harrison, John Harrison\'s son, who sailed with it: an Englishman of thirty-three with a long face and his own brown hair tied back, in a plain brown coat, standing at the ship\'s rail and holding H4 up in his open left palm at the height of his collar, just below and in front of his chin, looking down at it. H4 is one round silver case like a large pocket watch, a little wider than his palm and smaller than his face (his hand and his face are in true proportion: a hand is about as long as a face). It lies face up in his palm, tilted toward us: a white enamel dial with ornate dark hands and fine black scrollwork around its center, a silver ring at the top for its chain; there is no lid open above the dial and no works showing. Beside him, a step behind, one young naval officer in a dark blue coat holds a wooden octant up to take the sun\'s height: a triangular frame of dark wood about as long as his forearm, with a pale ivory scale along its curved lower edge and small brass peep sights, no telescope on it. Behind, the gray sea and rigging tied at both ends. Seen from a little farther back, from a little below, so that his whole head and shoulders are in the picture with plain sky above his head: the top of his head a sixth of the way down the picture, his face three fifths of the way across and a quarter of the way down, and the watch a third of the way down, well inside the upper half of the picture. Only two people. The watch face has no readable numbers or letters. No wristwatch, no digital display. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    stickchart: { cap: 'Marshall Islands, 1800s', ll: [171.38, 7.09], tx: 'Your Figures can walk two steps.', fact: 'Navigators of the Marshall Islands tied palm ribs and shells into charts of how ocean swells bend around islands. They learned them by heart on land, and at sea they steered by feeling the swells under the canoe.', art: 'A Marshallese stick chart about 1860, held up to the light by a navigator on the beach: an open frame of thin palm-rib sticks lashed together with coconut fiber, about as long as a man\'s arm: not a square grid, but a few long straight sticks that cross one another at different angles, several curved sticks bowing between them like the curves of swells around islands, and a few short sticks, all lashed at every crossing, with small white cowrie shells tied at a handful of the crossings to mark islands. The navigator, an older Marshallese man with a lined face and short gray hair, in a plain woven mat skirt, holds it while a young man of about seventeen beside him studies it. Behind, the lagoon, palms and an outrigger canoe with a triangular sail. He holds the chart flat and tilted toward us at the height of their chests, with both hands at its two ends, below their faces: both faces are fully clear above the chart, no stick crosses any face, and both men look down at it. The chart is wider than it is tall and stays well inside the picture, a fifth of the picture\'s width in from each side. Seen from in front and a little above, the two faces a fifth of the way down the picture with plain sky above them, and the chart just below them, both in the upper half of the picture. Only two people. The chart is one frame lashed at every crossing; no compass, no paper map, no feather headdresses. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    raingauge: { cap: 'Hanseong, 1441', ll: [126.98, 37.57], tx: 'Your Deeds cost 1 less.', fact: 'In 1441 and 1442 Korea sent a rain gauge of one standard size to every district, and had each rainfall measured and reported to the capital. It is the oldest known network for measuring rain anywhere.', art: 'A Korean rain gauge of the 1440s in the yard of a district office during a summer rain: a plain bronze cylinder as tall as a man\'s forearm and about as wide as a hand is long, standing in the round socket cut into the top of a carved granite block about knee high, with rain falling into it. A district clerk in a gray robe and a black gauze hat crouches and dips a thin ruler of wood marked with fine notches into it to read the depth, while one older official in a dark blue robe and a straw rain cape over his shoulders stands beside him in the rain, holding a brush and a folded paper ready under the shelter of his wide sleeve. No umbrella, no parasol. Behind, the tiled roof of the office and a wet courtyard. Seen from a little farther back and a little above, so the whole scene sits low and centered: the older official stands behind the gauge in the middle of the picture, his hat a sixth of the way down and his face a quarter of the way down, in the middle of the picture\'s width, with plain gray rain and roof eaves above him and nothing in either top corner; the clerk crouches at the left, his head a third of the way down; the whole bronze cylinder and the top of its granite block are in the upper half of the picture, the cylinder\'s mouth a little below the middle of its upper half, in the middle of the picture\'s width. The top fifth of the picture is gray falling rain and the eaves of the roof, nothing round. Only two people. No modern instruments, no Chinese or Japanese dress, no letters that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    codex: { cap: 'Yucatán, c. 1200', ll: [-88.57, 20.68], tx: 'At the start of your turn, before you draw, look at the top 3 cards of your draw pile and put them back in any order.', fact: 'One of only four Maya books to survive the Spanish burnings, it is folded like a screen from bark paper coated with lime. Its tables follow the planet Venus and warn of eclipses years ahead.', art: 'The Dresden Codex about 1200 in a stone room of a Maya city, opened by a Maya astronomer-priest at dawn: a long strip of pale bark paper folded like a screen, standing partly opened on a low stone bench, its pages painted with small red and black glyph blocks and dots and bars that can\'t be read as letters, and a painted figure of a god in each page\'s corner. The astronomer, a Maya man of about fifty with a sloping profile and a simple cloth headwrap, kneels and runs his finger along a column while he looks up through a narrow window at the morning star, Venus, bright above the horizon. Seen from beside the bench, the open pages and the star in the upper half, the book\'s center three fifths of the way across and a quarter of the way down. Only one person. No Aztec dress, no gold, no Western book binding. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    transit: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Jump one of your Figures to any Place. It can\'t act again this turn.', fact: 'On 3 June 1769 astronomers around the world timed the planet Venus as it crossed the face of the Sun, to work out how far away the Sun is. Cook\'s men watched it from a fort on Tahiti they named Point Venus.', art: 'The transit of Venus on 3 June 1769 at Point Venus on Tahiti: the astronomer of Cook\'s party, an Englishman of about thirty-five in shirtsleeves and a wide straw hat, sits on a stool at a short brass reflecting telescope on a wooden stand, its eyepiece fitted with a dark glass, watching the Sun, inside a low earth bank topped with a wooden fence. It is broad daylight: the sky is a pale cream haze with streaks of orange, not night and not a dark blue sky. The Sun is a flat bright cream disk with a thin orange rim, about a fifth of the picture\'s width across, standing in the open sky above the telescope, its center three fifths of the way across the picture and a third of the way down, well clear of the top edge and of both top corners. The small black round dot of Venus, about a twentieth of the Sun\'s width, sits just inside the Sun\'s lower edge. The telescope\'s tube points straight up at the Sun. Beside him a second man, older, in a plain coat, stands at the open flap of a canvas tent and counts the seconds from a tall wooden-cased pendulum clock inside it, writing in a notebook. Behind, the black-sand beach, palms and the masts of the Endeavour in the bay. Seen from beside the telescope, from low, the Sun with its dot and the astronomer\'s head both in the upper half of the picture. Only two people. No one looks at the Sun without the dark glass. No modern equipment. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    ether: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Enemy Figures at one Place miss their next turn.', fact: 'On 16 October 1846, in a Boston hospital, the dentist William Morton had a patient breathe ether, and the surgeon cut a lump from his neck while he slept. \'Gentlemen, this is no humbug,\' the surgeon is said to have told the doctors watching.', art: 'The first public operation under ether, 16 October 1846, in the steep round operating theater of the Massachusetts General Hospital in Boston: the patient, a young man, lies back asleep in a wooden operating chair, while William Morton, a dentist of twenty-seven with dark side whiskers, in a dark frock coat, holds a clear glass globe with two brass fittings and a mouthpiece to the patient\'s lips. Beside them the surgeon, John Collins Warren, an old man with white hair and a stern face, in a black coat, holds up a small knife and turns to speak to the doctors watching from the curved tiers of benches behind: three of them shown, each different, all three sitting on the lowest tier at the right, their heads a third of the way down the picture and smaller than the surgeon\'s. Above them the empty upper tiers curve away into shadow; nobody sits in the upper tiers, and no head is in the top quarter of the picture. Seen from the floor of the theater, Morton\'s face and the surgeon\'s face a fifth of the way down the picture, in the middle of its width, and the glass globe a third of the way down, all in the upper half; both top corners are empty dark benches. No blood shown, no modern masks or gloves. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    periodic: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Draw 2 cards.', fact: 'Mendeleev\'s table of 1869 put the sixty-three elements then known into rows and columns, so that elements that behave alike fall together. A version of it hangs in every chemistry classroom today.', art: 'Mendeleev\'s table in 1869, as a printed sheet pinned to the wall of a chemistry lecture room in Saint Petersburg and lit by a window: a large sheet of paper with a grid of small boxes, some boxes empty, each filled box holding a short mark too small to read. Before it a young student in a dark coat points to an empty box, and beside him a jar of silvery metal and a jar of yellow powder on a bench, with a glass flask over a burner. Seen from beside the bench, the sheet and the student\'s raised hand in the upper half, the sheet\'s center three fifths of the way across and a quarter of the way down. Only one person. No letters or numbers that can be read, no modern poster colors. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    paddle: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Choose a Domain where you have a Figure. Until this round ends, no opponent can start a clash with your Figures there.', fact: 'On a raid as a young chief, Kamehameha caught his foot in lava rock, and a fisherman broke a canoe paddle over his head. Years later, as king, he set the man free and made a law to protect the weak who travel the roads.', art: 'The moment behind the Law of the Splintered Paddle, on the lava coast of Puna in Hawaii: a young, powerfully built Hawaiian chief in a loincloth, one foot caught in a crack of black lava rock, raising an arm, while a fisherman, a lean older man in a loincloth, brings a wooden canoe paddle down on the chief\'s head and the paddle\'s blade splits apart, the splinters flying. Behind the fisherman, one young boy, his son, stands by a beached canoe. The sea and the surf behind. Seen from low on the rocks, the splintering paddle and the chief\'s head in the upper half, the paddle three fifths of the way across and a quarter of the way down. Only three people. No blood, no weapons other than the paddle, no feather cloaks. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    bachdang: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'One of your Figures starts a clash with an enemy Figure at its Place, with +1 Might, even if it has already acted.', fact: 'Trần Hưng Đạo\'s small boats drew the Yuan fleet up the Bạch Đằng River at high tide. As the tide went out, the big ships stuck fast on stakes hidden under the water, and hundreds of them were burned or taken.', art: 'The Battle of Bạch Đằng in 1288 as the tide falls: a big Yuan junk with ribbed square sails stuck fast and leaning, its hull caught fast on a dark wooden stake with an iron tip: the stake comes up out of the river bed under the junk and its tip has pierced the hull planks at the waterline, the stake showing only about a man\'s height above the falling brown water, lower than the junk\'s rail; more stakes, each leaning downstream, stand out in a broken line across the river. One small Vietnamese boat with two rowers, each different, slides past under the junk\'s side, one of them throwing a bundle of burning reeds. On the junk, two Yuan sailors look down in alarm. Seen from low on the water a little way off, the whole junk in the picture: the sailors at the rail a sixth of the way down, the point where the stake pierces the hull a third of the way down and three fifths of the way across, and the small boat with its two rowers just below it, all in the upper half. The water is brown and gray, with no red in it except the flame. Only four people. No rows of identical ships, no cannons, no European ships. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    malta: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Quick. When an enemy Figure would win a clash it started at your Gate or your Outpost and its own Gate has room, the Great Siege of Malta plays itself for 1 Spark you kept: the enemy Figure goes back to its Gate, and nobody is knocked out. With that Gate full, this card stays in your hand.', fact: 'In 1565 a huge Ottoman fleet landed an army on Malta, held by the Knights of Saint John and the Maltese. Fort Saint Elmo fell after a month, but the towns across the harbor held all summer, and in September, as help at last arrived, the attackers sailed away.', art: 'The Great Siege of Malta in 1565, on a stone bastion of Fort Saint Michael at dawn: a Maltese defender, a sturdy young man with a short beard in a plain shirt and leather jerkin, and one Knight of Saint John, an older man in a steel breastplate over a red surcoat with a white cross, push a long wooden ladder away from the wall with a forked pole, the ladder tipping backward, empty, toward the ditch below. Behind them the harbor and smoke from far guns. The bastion wall is one straight wall of cut limestone. Seen from beside them on the wall, both faces and the falling ladder in the upper half, the forked pole three fifths of the way across and a quarter of the way down. Only two people. No crowds of attackers, no blood shown, no later uniforms. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    thousand: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Play a Figure that costs 3 or less from your hand at any Place, without paying for it.', fact: 'In May 1860 Garibaldi\'s volunteers sailed from near Genoa in two paddle steamers and landed at Marsala in Sicily. By October they had won Sicily and Naples, and both voted to join a united Italy.', art: 'The landing at Marsala on 11 May 1860: a black paddle steamer with one tall funnel and a paddle box on its side lies close to the stone quay, and a rowing boat full of volunteers in red shirts comes ashore; on the quay two of them, each different, a young student and an older sailor, jump down with muskets while a Sicilian fisherman in a cap stares at them. Behind, the white town, the sea and a second steamer farther off. Seen from the quay, the steamer and the two volunteers in the upper half: their heads a quarter of the way down, between a third and two thirds of the way across, and the fisherman\'s head a third of the way down at the right; the funnel rises behind them, and nothing stands in the top corners. Three people near us, the rest small and few in the boat, each different. No warships, no rows of identical soldiers. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    fourseasons: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'One of your Figures that can\'t act right now can act this turn.', fact: 'Vivaldi\'s four violin concertos, printed in 1725, paint the year in sound: birdsong, a summer storm, a harvest dance and skaters slipping on ice. Each one came with a poem telling what the music shows.', art: 'The Four Seasons played about 1725 in a Venetian music room: a young violinist of the Pietà, a girl of about sixteen in a plain dark dress, plays standing by a tall window, her bow flying, and beside her the window has four tall panes side by side; through each pane the lagoon is seen in a different season, as if her music painted it, from left to right: spring blossom, a summer thunderstorm, autumn vines with grapes, and winter ice at the water\'s edge. One older woman plays a cello beside her. Seen from inside the room, the violinist\'s face and the window in the upper half, her head three fifths of the way across and a quarter of the way down. Only two people. The violin rests on her left shoulder under her chin, her left hand on its neck and the bow in her right hand; the violin has four strings, and the cello stands on the floor between its player\'s knees. No letters on the music that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    nightwatch: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Add +4 Might to your total at a Place where you have a Figure, until the round ends.', fact: 'Rembrandt painted Captain Banninck Cocq\'s company of Amsterdam\'s guard setting out, not standing in rows as group portraits usually did. The picture was so big, over four meters wide, that in 1715 it was cut down to fit between two doors.', art: 'The painting of The Night Watch in 1642, seen in Rembrandt\'s studio as he finishes it: the huge canvas stands on the floor, taller than two men, showing a captain in black with a red sash and a lieutenant in pale yellow striding forward out of shadow among guardsmen with pikes and a drum; in front of it Rembrandt, a painter of thirty-six with a broad face, a small mustache and a dark beret, stands on a low wooden step at the right, turned three-quarters toward us so his face shows in profile, his palette in his left hand and in his raised right hand one ordinary paintbrush, as long as his forearm, its tip touching the lieutenant\'s pale yellow coat; nothing else in his hands, and no pike or line in the painting runs on from the brush. Seen from beside him, the painting and his raised arm in the upper half, his head three fifths of the way across and a quarter of the way down. Only Rembrandt living; the painted figures are clearly paint, few and each different. No frame, no museum. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    gitanjali: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Gain 3 Spark.', fact: 'Tagore\'s \'song offerings\', which he turned from Bengali into English himself, were printed in London in 1912. The next year they won him the Nobel Prize in Literature, the first given to a writer from outside Europe.', art: 'Gitanjali in 1912: Rabindranath Tagore, a Bengali poet of fifty-one with long wavy gray hair and a long gray beard, in a loose dark robe, sits in a deck chair on a steamship crossing to England with a small writing board across the chair\'s arms at the height of his chest; on the board lie two open notebooks side by side, the Bengali one at his left, which he reads, and the English one at his right, in which he writes with a pen; the sea and sky behind him. Seen from beside him and a little in front, his face and the two notebooks close below it both in the upper half of the picture, his head three fifths of the way across and a quarter of the way down. Only him. A respectful, recognizable likeness from his photographs. No letters that can be read. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    krakatoa: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Choose a Domain. Every Figure there, on every side, misses its next turn.', fact: 'In August 1883 the volcano of Krakatoa, between Java and Sumatra, blew itself apart. The bang was heard nearly 5,000 kilometers away, and its dust turned sunsets red around the world for months.', art: 'The eruption of Krakatoa on 27 August 1883, seen from the deck of a small Dutch sailing ship in the Sunda Strait: a towering column of dark ash and fire rises from the island, filling the sky, lightning flickering inside it, while a wave rolls toward the ship. On the deck one Javanese sailor and one Dutch captain, each different, hold to the rigging and stare. Seen from the deck, the ash column and the two men\'s faces in the upper half, the column three fifths of the way across and reaching above a quarter of the way down; the top corners are dark ash cloud only. Only two people. One ash column only, not mirrored or repeated. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    panama: { cap: 'Deed', tx: 'Swap two Figures at different Places. At least one must be yours.', fact: 'In 1914 ships began to cross from the Atlantic to the Pacific through Panama, lifted over the hills by locks and lowered again on the far side. Thousands died building it, most from disease, until doctors fought the mosquitoes.', art: 'The Panama Canal in 1914: a steamship with one tall funnel rides high in a vast concrete lock chamber, coming toward us, the two great steel gates of the lock standing open behind it, each gate as tall as a six-story building, while a small electric towing engine on rails on the lock wall pulls it forward by a cable tied at both ends. On the wall a lock worker in a white shirt and hat, and one Panamanian boy beside him, watch. Green jungle hills beyond. Seen from the lock wall, the ship\'s bow and the open gates in the upper half, the gap between the gates three fifths of the way across and a quarter of the way down. Only two people. The lock is one chamber in one perspective, its two walls straight and parallel; the towing engine\'s rails run along the flat top of the near wall, where the two people stand, and nothing rises beside the track: past the people there is only the edge of the wall and the hills. The water stands high in the chamber, a little below the top of the walls. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    hunayn: { cap: 'Baghdad, c. 809 to 873', ll: [44.37, 33.31], tx: 'Scholar: draw a card when this Figure is played.', fact: 'A Christian doctor from al-Hira, he and his students put more than a hundred of Galen\'s medical books into Syriac and Arabic for the doctors of Baghdad. He gathered several Greek copies of a book and compared them before he translated it.', art: 'Hunayn ibn Ishaq about 860 in his workroom in Baghdad, translating: a lean Arab Christian scholar of about fifty with a trimmed gray beard and a plain dark cap, in a long dark robe, sitting cross-legged on a low platform with three old Greek manuscripts open in front of him side by side, one finger on a line of one of them while he reads it aloud. Beside him a young assistant, his son, in a lighter robe, writes the Arabic on a long sheet on a writing board on his knee. A shelf of rolled and bound books behind them, a brass lamp, a high latticed window. Seen from the front, a little from above, Hunayn\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the three manuscripts in the upper half. Only two people. The writing is small marks that cannot be read. No printed books, no spectacles. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    cherschel: { cap: 'Bath and Slough, 1750 to 1848', ll: [-0.59, 51.51], tx: 'When you play this Figure, you gain 1 Spark.', fact: 'She swept the night sky with a small telescope of her own and found eight comets. In 1787 King George III gave her fifty pounds a year as her brother William\'s assistant, the first woman in Britain paid for work in science.', art: 'Caroline Herschel about 1787 on a clear night in the garden at Slough, sweeping for comets: a small, slight German woman of thirty-seven with her hair pinned up under a plain white cap, a shawl over a dark wool dress, standing at her small reflecting telescope, her comet sweeper. It is a Newtonian reflector and is drawn that way: a long, slim, square wooden tube as long as her arm, tilted steeply up toward the sky, held at its middle in a wooden fork on a short wooden post stand that she turns by a handle. Its lower end, closed, is down at the height of her waist, a little in front of her; its upper end is an open square mouth facing the sky, just above and beside her head. Her eye is at a short brass eyepiece that sticks straight out sideways from the side of the tube a hand\'s width below that open mouth, at the height of her eye, and she looks into it from the side, her face turned toward the tube\'s side at right angles to it. Nothing is at the tube\'s lower end and she is not looking along the tube. On a small table beside her a candle in a glass lantern lights an open notebook and a pen. Behind her, dark trees and the corner of a brick house with one lit window, and far off, small against the stars, the tall triangular timber scaffold (far smaller than the house) of poles and ladders that held William\'s much bigger telescope, its long tube leaning in it. Seen from beside her, a little from below, her head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the telescope and the starry sky in the upper half. Only her. No modern telescope, no metal tripod, no electric light. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    synesius: { cap: 'Cyrene, c. 370 to c. 413', ll: [21.86, 32.82], tx: '', fact: 'A landowner of Cyrene who studied in Alexandria, he went to Constantinople to plead with the emperor for lower taxes on his province. He later became a bishop, and in one letter asked Hypatia to have a brass hydrometer made for him.', art: 'Synesius of Cyrene about 400 in his country house near Cyrene, writing a letter: a lean, dark-haired Greek of Libya of about thirty with a short beard, in a plain pale tunic and a dark cloak, sitting at a small wooden table under a window and writing on a sheet of papyrus with a reed pen, and beside the papyrus lies an open wax tablet with a simple sketch scratched on it of the instrument he is asking for: a slender upright tube with notches down its side and a weighted cone at its foot. Through the window, dry hills, an olive tree and a hunting dog asleep in the sun. Seen from the side of the table, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the papyrus and the wax tablet with its sketch in the upper half. Only him. The writing is small marks that cannot be read. No bishop\'s robes, no crosses, no printed books, no finished instrument on the table, no glass instruments. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    revere: { cap: 'Boston, 1735 to 1818', ll: [-71.06, 42.36], tx: 'Swift: can walk on the turn it\'s played.', fact: 'A Boston silversmith, he rode with the news for the town\'s patriot committees. On the night of 18 April 1775 he rode to Lexington to warn that British troops were on the march. He was caught on the road to Concord, but Samuel Prescott got through.', art: 'Paul Revere on the night of 18 April 1775 riding through the countryside outside Boston: a solid, broad-faced man of forty, a recognizable likeness from Copley\'s portrait of him (a round, strong face, dark eyes under heavy dark brows, a firm chin, clean-shaven) with his own dark hair tied back, no wig, in a dark wool coat, a plain waistcoat and riding boots, leaning forward on a brown horse at a gallop along a dirt road between a stone wall and a farmhouse, turning his head with his mouth open, calling out toward the farmhouse at the right, where in one lit window halfway up the picture a farmer in a nightshirt leans out holding a candle. The window is in the middle of the picture\'s height, well below the top right corner. A bright moon, nearly full, low over bare spring trees in the middle of the sky, not in a corner. The horse is much bigger than the man: its back is as high as a standing man\'s chest, and Revere sits small on it, his feet in stirrups; it has four legs in a true gallop. Seen from the roadside, a little below, Revere\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the window and the moon in the upper half. Only two people. No lantern in a church tower in the picture, no modern road. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    banzhao: { cap: 'Luoyang, c. 45 to c. 117', ll: [112.45, 34.62], tx: 'Inspiring: your other Figures here get +1 Might.', fact: 'When her brother Ban Gu died with his history of the Han dynasty unfinished, the emperor had her complete it. She taught the empress and the ladies of the palace, and scholars came to her to learn to read the history.', art: 'Ban Zhao about 106 in a room of the Han palace at Luoyang, teaching: a dignified Chinese woman of about sixty with gray hair pinned up under a simple dark headdress, in a long layered silk robe of dark red and black with wide sleeves, kneeling at a low lacquered table and pointing with a brush to a column on an unrolled bundle of bamboo strips, while two young women of the court, each different in age and dress, kneel across the table and follow her brush. All three wear Han dynasty dress as the tomb figurines and paintings of the period show it: a long robe wrapped round the body with a crossed collar, plain or with a small woven pattern, long wide sleeves, a narrow sash; the young women\'s hair gathered in a low knot at the nape of the neck. No kimono, no wide obi sash, no floral prints, no dangling hairpins. Painted wooden pillars, a folding screen, a bronze lamp. Seen from across the table, Ban Zhao\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, her brush and the strips in the upper half. Only three people. The marks on the strips cannot be read. No paper books, no later dynasties\' dress, no fans or parasols. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    fadlan: { cap: 'Baghdad to the Volga, 921 to 922', ll: [49.06, 54.98], tx: 'Voyager: can walk to any Place.', fact: 'In 921 he set out from Baghdad as secretary of the caliph\'s embassy to the king of the Volga Bulgars, almost a year\'s journey. His account describes the Rus traders he met there, and a chieftain\'s funeral in a burning ship.', art: 'Ibn Fadlan in 922 on the bank of the Volga, writing: an Arab scholar of about forty with a black beard, a white turban and a long traveling coat lined with fur, sitting on a folded rug on the riverbank with a writing case on his knee, his reed pen raised as he looks up from the page at what he sees. Beyond him on the wide gray river lie two wooden trading boats drawn up on the shore, and one Rus trader, a tall man with a short beard and a cloak pinned at the shoulder, unloads a bundle of furs. Birch woods on the far bank, a cold pale sky. Seen from behind his shoulder and to one side, his face turned so we see it, his head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, the river and the boats in the upper half. Only two people. No horned helmets, no film costumes, no fire. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
    larrey: { cap: 'France, 1766 to 1842', ll: [2.35, 48.86], tx: 'Enemies can\'t clash with this Figure while a stronger Figure of yours is here.', fact: 'Army surgeons used to wait behind the lines until a battle was over. Larrey\'s flying ambulances, light carts on springs, went out under fire to bring the wounded in at once, and he treated wounded enemies like his own men.', art: 'Dominique-Jean Larrey about 1797 on a battlefield in Italy, bringing in the wounded: a French army surgeon of thirty with long dark hair, a lean, determined face and no wig, in a dark blue coat with a high collar, kneeling on the ground beside a wounded young soldier in a white uniform of the other side, tying a bandage round his arm. Behind them stands a light, closed two-wheeled box carriage on leather and iron springs, painted dark green, pulled by two horses, its rear door folded down over a mattress floor, and one orderly in a plain coat helps a second wounded man up into it. Smoke drifts over a far hillside. Seen from low beside the wounded soldier, Larrey\'s head three fifths of the way across the picture and a quarter of the way down, his hands tying the bandage in the upper half too; the carriage stands to one side behind him, its roof a sixth of the way down, not in a corner. Only four people. No red cross anywhere, no modern stretcher, no blood pools. Keep the top left corner and the top right corner of the picture plain: the card\'s own labels cover those two corners. Nothing round hangs or stands in those corners: no plates, dishes, bowls or medallions.' },
  };
