    "harun>zubaidah": "Zubaidah married her cousin Harun al-Rashid, and as his queen she had the wealth to build her road.",
    "suleiman>sinan": "Suleiman made Sinan his chief architect in 1538, and Sinan built him the Süleymaniye Mosque.",
    "@timbuktu>leoafricanus": "Leo Africanus saw Timbuktu about 1510, and wrote that more profit was made there from selling books than from any other goods.",
    "@constantinople>greekfire": "Greek fire first saved Constantinople when an Arab fleet besieged it in the 670s.",
    "sejong>raingauge": "In Sejong's reign, in 1441 and 1442, his court made a rain gauge of one standard size and sent it to every district of Korea.",
    "cook>transit": "The Admiralty sent Cook to Tahiti in the Endeavour to watch the transit of Venus.",
    "mendeleev>periodic": "Mendeleev sent his first table of the elements to chemists across Europe in March 1869.",
    "kamehameha>paddle": "Kamehameha proclaimed the Law of the Splintered Paddle in 1797, in memory of the blow he had taken.",
    "tranhungdao>bachdang": "Trần Hưng Đạo set his trap on the Bạch Đằng, the river where Ngô Quyền had caught a fleet on stakes in 938.",
    "garibaldi>thousand": "Garibaldi led the Thousand himself, from the sailing at Quarto to his entry into Naples.",
    "vivaldi>fourseasons": "Vivaldi printed The Four Seasons in 1725 with a poem for each concerto, perhaps written by himself.",
    "rembrandt>nightwatch": "Captain Banninck Cocq's company paid Rembrandt to paint them for the great hall of their guild in Amsterdam.",
    "tagore>gitanjali": "Tagore began turning his Bengali songs into English while recovering from illness in 1912, and finished on the voyage to London.",
    "hippocrates>hunayn": "Hunayn ibn Ishaq translated the books of Hippocrates into Arabic, with Galen's commentaries on them.",
    "hypatia>synesius": "Synesius studied under Hypatia in Alexandria, and in his last letter to her he called her his mother, his sister and his teacher.",
    "napoleon>larrey": "In his will Napoleon left Larrey a hundred thousand francs and called him the most virtuous man he had known.",
    "hafez>goethe": "Goethe read Hafez in a new German translation in 1814, called him his twin, and answered him with a whole book of poems, the West-Eastern Divan.",
    "peter>catherine": "Catherine raised the Bronze Horseman to Peter in 1782, inscribed 'To Peter the First from Catherine the Second'.",
    "vivaldi>bach": "At Weimar in 1713 and 1714 Bach rewrote Vivaldi's concertos for keyboard and organ, and learned from them how to build his own.",
    "tycho>kepler": "Kepler worked out the paths of the planets from the observations Tycho Brahe had gathered over twenty years.",
